Goto Naviagation&#x1f447; English Sadguru Muktanand Swami&rsquo;s Gentle Counsel, a Sadhu&rsquo;s Anger, and Swami&rsquo;s Magnanimous Forgiveness The adage &lsquo;A noble life inspires noble lives&rsquo; was vividly embodied by Sadguru Muktanand Swami, celebrated as the compassionate mother of the Satsang fellowship. Whenever spiritual refinement was required, he never hesitated to offer gentle reprimands to nurture young monks into exemplary ascetics.
On one occasion, Sadguru Muktanand Swami arrived in a village accompanied by a assembly of sants. Some sadhus went to the nearby river to bathe. Upon returning to the mandir, a young sadhu stood right at the edge of the assembly hall&rsquo;s veranda and began washing his feet. The water splashed directly onto the woven carpets (modiyu) laid out for the congregation. At that very moment, Muktanand Swami was delivering a holy discourse to a devoted gathering of villagers. Observing the floor mats getting soaked, Muktanand Swami gently corrected him: &ldquo;Sadhuram! Shall I fetch a basin for you to wash your feet? The assembly carpet is getting soaked, yet you persist! Pray exercise basic discretion and wash your feet aside. Such heedless conduct does not befit an ascetic. Every action of a sadhu should be so noble and dignified that both believers and non-believers are inspired toward virtue. Witnessing this careless action, what will these devotees learn?&rdquo;
Though spoken with pure maternal affection, Muktanand Swami&rsquo;s reprimand felt like a blazing ember to the young sadhu&rsquo;s fragile ego. Smoldering with fury, he retreated into an inner chamber and harbored a sinister urge to avenge the perceived slight. Pretending to be unwell, he tossed restlessly under his blanket, waiting for the dark hours of midnight.
When the entire monastery fell silent and the monks were asleep, the young sadhu crept over and awoke Muktanand Swami: &ldquo;Swami! I need to relieve myself outside the village outskirts; please accompany me.&rdquo;
Muktanand Swami replied with calm gentleness: &ldquo;Sadhuram! It is past midnight. It is improper for sadhus to wander outside the village in the dead of night, as it invites public misunderstanding. Kindly use the facility in the mandir backyard and rest on the veranda. In the morning, you may go out to the well for your ablutions, and I will gladly accompany you.&rdquo;
Despite Swami&rsquo;s gentle reasoning, the young sadhu stubbornly insisted on walking out to the secluded well. Muktanand Swami gazed deeply into the monk&rsquo;s face. Illuminated by the dim lamp, the monk&rsquo;s face was flushed red with wrath, and his breath was trembling. Being spiritually perceptive, Swami instantly discerned his lethal motive: &lsquo;This monk has no need for the fields; he intends to push me into the well and take my life!&rsquo;
Far from taking offense or feeling afraid, the saintly embodiment of compassion lovingly seated the troubled young man beside him. Caressing his back with motherly affection, Muktanand Swami imparted profound spiritual wisdom regarding the sanctity of renunciation, the poison of ego and anger, and the sublime power of forbearance. Soaked in unconditional love, the soothing counsel melted the monk&rsquo;s wrath, and he quietly went to sleep.
At daybreak, profound remorse overwhelmed the young monk: &lsquo;Merciful heavens! Had Sadguru Muktanand Swami accompanied me last night, my hands would have been stained with the murder of an enlightened, God-realized saint! For countless lifetimes, I would have languished in the darkest depths of hell!&rsquo;
Shaking violently with horror and sorrow, he rushed to Muktanand Swami, fell prostrate at his lotus feet, and wept uncontrollably: &ldquo;O Swami! Had you accompanied me to the well last night, I had resolved to cast you into its depths and kill you! I am a vile offender; please, forgive my unforgivable sin!&rdquo;
Without a trace of bitterness, the ocean of forgiveness gently raised him up and declared: &ldquo;Sadhuram, you are completely forgiven. False pride and transient anger had clouded your intellect, inciting such destructive impulses. Cast aside your distress, remain vigilant against ego, and devote your mind purely to the worship of God.&rdquo; Through his supreme patience and forgiving nature, Sadguru Muktanand Swami illustrated the pinnacle of saintliness.
- From &lsquo;Four Sadgurus—Editors of Vachanamrut&rsquo;&hellip;
