Goto Naviagation&#x1f447; English Sadguru Dhyananand Swami&rsquo;s Resolute Conviction, Profound Humility, and Shreeji Maharaj&rsquo;s Divine Grace at Vadtal Sadguru Dhyananand Swami was born in the village of Chhapi in the Vadgam region. Endowed with spiritual virtues from previous births, he had been an ardent seeker of the manifest Supreme Lord since childhood. Upon meeting Shreeji Maharaj, he embraced paramhansa initiation, receiving the monastic name Dhyananand Swami.
During a tumultuous period in Ahmedabad caused by the hostility of Lolangar Bawa, Shreeji Maharaj dispatched Sadguru Muktanand Swami to Surat with a large congregation of 150 sants. Maharaj had issued a strict spiritual injunction forbidding conversation with hostile or faithless people. Enduring severe austerities and illnesses in an orchard outside Surat, the monks remained steadfast, though three prominent ascetics—Akhilbrahmandeshwaranand Swami, Trivikramanand Swami, and Prabhavanand Swami—departed for Akshardham during this arduous campaign.
Dhyananand Swami was part of this contingent. By nature, he possessed an exceptionally fierce, volatile, and impatient temperament. Dominated by his fiery disposition, he often broke away from the monastic group to wander in solitude. Yet, his faith in the supreme divinity of Bhagwan Swaminarayan was so deeply ingrained that even if he walked twenty miles away, the moment he found no one chanting the holy Swaminarayan Mahamantra, his soul would yearn intensely, compelling him to hurry back to where the sants were congregated. Upon his return, he would sit quietly in the back during Muktanand Swami&rsquo;s spiritual assemblies.
Adhering strictly to Shreeji Maharaj&rsquo;s disciplinary command, Muktanand Swami addressed him firmly: &ldquo;Dhyananand Swami! We are under strict orders from Maharaj not to converse with you!&rdquo;
Heartbroken, Dhyananand Swami broke into tears and folded his hands in supreme contrition: &ldquo;O Swami! If you command, I will stand far away where the discarded footwear of the sants is piled, and gaze upon you from afar; but pray do not cast me out! Unless I am blessed with the sight and association of Shreeji Maharaj&rsquo;s sants and devotees, my soul can find no peace anywhere in this universe!&rdquo;
Touched by his profound yearning, Muktanand Swami, renowned as the compassionate mother of the fellowship, granted him permission to stand at a distance near the footwear. Anchored in steadfast conviction and humble repentance, Dhyananand Swami stood beside the shoes and addressed all passing visitors: &ldquo;Behold these holy sants! They are like spotless platters of pure gold, whereas I am merely a rusted iron spike! Seek their holy company and surrender to Bhagwan Swaminarayan!&rdquo; By continuously extolling the glory of the sants and the supreme divinity of Shreeji Maharaj, he inspired numerous worldly individuals to embrace Satsang. Witnessing his pure service, the entire sant mandal was delighted, and when they traveled to Vadtal to meet Shreeji Maharaj, they affectionately took Dhyananand Swami along.
In Vadtal, Shreeji Maharaj was seated under the cooling shade of tamarind trees on the banks of Gomti Lake, holding a divine assembly. Dhyananand Swami rushed forward, threw himself prostrate upon the ground from a distance, and wept inconsolably: &ldquo;O Maharaj! Because of my volatile nature, I am unfit to dwell among these exalted sants. They are like golden platters and I am like a rusted iron nail, bringing disgrace to this holy order. Pray shatter my spiritual conviction in You so that I may take this blackened face and perish in some secluded wilderness!&rdquo;
Hearing his desperate lamentation, Shreeji Maharaj turned to Muktanand Swami: &ldquo;Swami, what is he lamenting?&rdquo;
Muktanand Swami testified with profound warmth: &ldquo;Maharaj! His conviction in Your divine supremacy is unshakeable. When We forbade him from joining the inner assembly per Your discipline, he stood humbly by the footwear outside and passionately proclaimed Your divine glory to passersby, transforming countless non-believers into steadfast devotees!&rdquo;
Hearing this, Shreeji Maharaj was overjoyed. Beckoning Dhyananand Swami close to His cot, Maharaj placed His sacred hands upon the monk&rsquo;s head and blessed him: &ldquo;Dhyananand Swami! From this day forth, dwell peacefully within the assembly of sants!&rdquo;
With folded hands, Dhyananand Swami pleaded: &ldquo;O Supreme Redeemer! In Your infinite mercy You accept me again and again, but my turbulent temperament constantly undermines me!&rdquo;
With a compassionate smile, Shreeji Maharaj pronounced His sovereign boon: &ldquo;Be assured, Dhyananand! We grant you Our solemn word that from this moment onward, your innate temperament shall never again obstruct your spiritual peace!&rdquo;
Instantly sanctified by the Lord&rsquo;s blessing, Dhyananand Swami&rsquo;s volatile nature dissolved into serene tranquility. He lived in harmonious bliss within the brotherhood, and two and a half months later, shed his mortal coil to enter eternal Akshardham. He was also a gifted poet whose devotional verses celebrating the divine glory of Shreeji Maharaj continue to inspire seekers.
In Bhaktachintamani (Prakaran 52), Sadguru Nishkulanand Swami immortalized him among the celebrated paramhansas:
Dheeranand Drishtaprakashanand, Dhyananand dhyan dhare; Prabhanand ne Purushanand, Sankhyanand anand kare..!!
- From Bhaktachintamani and the discourses of Sadguru Adbhutanand Swami&hellip;
