Goto Naviagation&#x1f447; English The Ordinance of One Extra Dandavat at Gangajaliyo Well in Gadhpur and the Mystery of Vachanamrut Gadhada Madhya 40 On the sacred morning of Samvat 1880, Aso Vad 3 (October 22, 1823), Bhagwan Swaminarayan was bathing near the celebrated Gangajaliyo well in Gadhpur. Between two rooms, His attendant Parsad Ratanji had laid out a woven grass mat covered with a soft woolen blanket, arranging the sacred images of Thakorji for Shreeji Maharaj&rsquo;s morning worship. Sitting close by observing the proceedings were Sadguru Nityanand Swami, Shukanand Swami, and Atmanand Swami.
After completing His ablutions, Shreeji Maharaj commenced His daily worship and offered prostrations (dandavat pranam) before the deities. Observing with keen attention, Shukmuni leaned over and whispered to Nityanand Swami: &ldquo;Shreeji Maharaj traditionally performs five dandavats every morning; why did He offer one extra dandavat today?&rdquo;
Overhearing their whisper, the omniscient Lord inquired: &ldquo;What is Shukmuni saying?&rdquo;
Nityanand Swami folded his hands and submitted: &ldquo;O Maharaj! Shukmuni is inquiring why Maharaj performed one extra dandavat today.&rdquo;
Shreeji Maharaj revealed the deep spiritual motive behind His action: &ldquo;Listen, let Me explain. Today We went to visit Alaiya Khachar. Because he harbored contempt, jealousy, and ill-will toward holy sants and devotees, he is suffering such agonizing inner torment as We have never witnessed in anyone before! Harboring malice toward God&rsquo;s devotees has brought him unimaginable spiritual agony!&rdquo; (Discourses of Bhayatmanand Swami: 326)
This momentous event forms the historical core of Vachanamrut Gadhada Madhya 40, faithfully recorded by the compiler sants. Addressing the gathering, Shreeji Maharaj instructed: &ldquo;Daily, while bowing to Bhagwan, We used to pray: &lsquo;O Lord! Eradicate whatever ego and worldly attachment reside within this body.&rsquo; But today, the realization struck Us: &lsquo;The agony experienced by a soul who—knowingly or unknowingly, through thought, word, or deed—harbors malice or offends a devotee of God, is far more excruciating than the torment caused by any other sin!&rsquo; Therefore, to atone for and dissolve any inadvertent offense toward a devotee of God in thought, word, or deed, We offered one extra prostration today.
Furthermore, so that this ordinance and solemn principle remain etched in everyone&rsquo;s memory daily, let all sants and devotees adopt this binding discipline starting today: &lsquo;After completing the routine prostrations during daily morning puja, perform one additional dandavat specifically to seek atonement for any offense—conscious or unconscious, through thought, speech, or physical action—committed against any devotee of God throughout the day.&rsquo; This is Our divine injunction, and all must adhere to it diligently.&rdquo;
In the scriptural epic &lsquo;Shree Haricharitramrut Sagar&rsquo;, Sadguru Aadharanand Swami specifically recorded that Shreeji Maharaj previously performed five dandavats during His daily personal worship. Following this divine revelation, He added one more, consistently performing six prostrations daily thereafter.
By exemplifying this profound virtue Himself, Bhagwan Swaminarayan underscored the devastating peril of harboring malice toward fellow devotees, establishing a permanent daily ritual of humility and reconciliation for spiritual seekers.
- From &lsquo;Four Sadgurus—Editors of Vachanamrut&rsquo;&hellip;
