Goto Naviagation&#x1f447; English The Letter of Khagol-Bhugol in Gadhpur&rsquo;s Bhaktibag, Rarity of the Human Body, and Shukmuni&rsquo;s Astute Guidance On the holy festival day of Bhadarva Sud 11, Samvat 1873, Bhagwan Swaminarayan sat under the soothing shade of the Harma tree in the Bhaktibag orchard of Gadhada. There, He dictated an extraordinary philosophical and cosmological epistle to His beloved scribe, Sadguru Shukanand Swami (Shukmuni). Describing the intricate structure of the cosmos (Khagol-Bhugol) and the supreme rarity of attaining a human body in Bharatkhand, this celebrated writing is revered across the fellowship as the supplementary &lsquo;Khagol-Bhugol Vachanamrut&rsquo;.
A human birth in Bharatkhand is attained after passing through three and a half crore spans of Brahma; If even then one seeks the company of true sants, one crosses the ocean of worldly existence; Otherwise, one plunges once again into hell, wandering endlessly through eighty-four lakh species; Therefore, awake and realize, O brother, this auspicious opportunity has arrived! Contemplate the sacred scriptures, heed the instruction of the enlightened Guru; Strive earnestly for ultimate liberation, or endless suffering shall follow!!
Dictating this letter, Shreeji Maharaj stressed to Shukanand Swami: &ldquo;The soul who attains this precious human birth yet fails to secure eternal liberation is deemed the greatest fool by enlightened sages and scriptures. No matter how astute or prosperous he may be in worldly commerce, if he neglects his soul&rsquo;s salvation, he is as worthless as dry straw. Woe to his intellect, his wealth, his talents, and his very human existence if he ruins his spiritual redemption! Sensual pleasures are effortlessly enjoyed even by dogs, donkeys, and pigs; they are not rare. What is truly rare is this human form, genuine spiritual yearning, the fellowship of holy sants, and the direct refuge of the manifest Supreme Lord. Therefore, let the wise dedicate themselves to divine worship and ensure their eternal liberation.&rdquo;
In Vachanamrut Gadhada Pratham 50, Shreeji Maharaj articulates this exact truth: &ldquo;One who strives earnestly for his spiritual redemption, even if possessing modest intellectual ability, is truly sharp-witted. Conversely, one who is obsessively vigilant in worldly affairs with great sharp-wittedness, yet indifferent to his salvation, is essentially dull-witted. A true devotee&rsquo;s intellect remains completely blind to sensual indulgence. In essence, he who secures his eternal liberation is truly wise, while he who squanders it is a consummate fool.&rdquo;
A parallel incident reflects the practical wisdom of Sadguru Shukanand Swami. A devout householder came to Gadhada to reside with Shukanand Swami for spiritual association. Within a few days, however, the devotee began having persistent dreams of his wife back home! Perplexed and distressed, he approached Shukanand Swami with disarming candor: &ldquo;Swami! When I lived at home amidst domestic life, I repeatedly dreamed of you and the holy sadhus. But now that I have come here to live among renunciants, I dream of my wife every single night! What should I do? I shall do exactly as you command.&rdquo;
Hearing this honest confession, the spiritually astute Shukmuni smiled gently and counseled: &ldquo;Brother! Return home immediately! When you live in domestic life and dream of sadhus, your spiritual salvation progresses. But if you dwell here among ascetics and dream of worldly attachments, your spiritual well-being will be compromised! Therefore, dwell peacefully within the householder&rsquo;s dharma, perform sincere devotion, and visit the fellowship during grand festivals to absorb spiritual discourses. That way your heart will remain pure and your liberation assured.&rdquo;
Accepting Swami&rsquo;s practical wisdom, the devotee returned home, faithfully practiced his spiritual disciplines, and was soon blessed with unbroken divine contemplation of Shreeji Maharaj.
- From &lsquo;Shree Harililamrutam&rsquo; and &lsquo;Four Sadgurus—Editors of Vachanamrut&rsquo;&hellip;
