Goto Naviagation&#x1f447; English Purushottamdas&rsquo;s Worldly Attachment in Rojka and Shivshankar Bhatt&rsquo;s Ingenious Device to Sever Delusion In the village of Rojka lived an affluent Brahmin named Purushottamdas Khakhdas. Well-provided with worldly prosperity and financial means, he had nevertheless remained unmarried throughout his youth. Consequently, a deep-seated, persistent worldly desire (vasana) for domestic companionship and marriage occupied his thoughts. Eventually, his relatives arranged his betrothal to a woman in the village of Juna Savar near Savarkundla.
By then, Purushottamdas was advanced in years. Even before the wedding ceremonies could take place, he was struck by a severe and debilitating illness. Having already paid substantial wedding funds to the bride&rsquo;s household, his health deteriorated rapidly. As death drew near, his fevered mind succumbed to delirious anxiety. Tossing restlessly on his cot, he lamented aloud: &ldquo;Alas! My hard-earned wealth is gone, and I could not even take the wedding vows around the sacred fire! Woe is me!&rdquo;
He writhed upon his cot, continuously venting his unfulfilled worldly infatuation. Living in the same village was a wise, spiritually mature Brahmin devotee named Shivshankar Bhatt. Learning of Purushottamdas&rsquo;s pitiful state, Shivshankar reflected deeply: &lsquo;Purushottamdas is an earnest devotee, yet at this critical juncture of death, this obsessive marital attachment will cause his spiritual ruin. If his soul departs entangled in this mundane delusion, he will inevitably plunge back into the cycle of eighty-four lakh rebirths! I must devise an ingenious remedy to sever this worldly attachment immediately.&rsquo;
With purposeful resolve, Shivshankar slung a traveler&rsquo;s cloth bag over his shoulder, feigning that he had just returned from a long journey, and hurried to Purushottamdas&rsquo;s residence. Stepping briskly to the side of his cot, he called out loudly: &ldquo;Purushottamdasji! Jai Swaminarayan!&rdquo;
Opening his weary eyes, Purushottamdas asked feebly: &ldquo;Shivshankar! From where are you returning?&rdquo;
With fabricated gravity, Shivshankar replied: &ldquo;I have been traveling extensively across the Kathiyawad region, and have returned directly from there.&rdquo;
Hearing the name Kathiyawad, Purushottamdas&rsquo;s face lit up with anxious curiosity: &ldquo;Brother Shivshankar! My betrothal was fixed in Juna Savar; do you bring any tidings from there?&rdquo;
Seizing the opportune moment, Shivshankar cast his strategic bait: &ldquo;It is precisely for that reason that I hurried directly to your side! But inquiring while lying comfortably on your cot violates scriptural decorum for a departing soul. Let your family members lift you off the cot and place you upon the sanctified floor; only then will I deliver the news!&rdquo;
Adhering to the sacred custom of placing a dying person upon the hallowed earth, the household members gently transferred the terminally ill man from the cot onto the floor. Trembling with impatience, Purushottamdas urged: &ldquo;Speak, Shivshankar! What is the news?&rdquo;
Adopting an expression of grim sorrow, Shivshankar delivered the shocking thunderbolt: &ldquo;Purushottamdas! That prospective bride for whom you are burning your soul has suddenly passed away!&rdquo;
Hearing those fateful words, an electric jolt struck Purushottamdas&rsquo;s heart. In a single instant, the entire illusion of romantic domesticity that had enslaved his mind for years shattered completely. In utter shock, his lips gasped: &ldquo;What&hellip;!!&rdquo;
And with that single breathless exclamation, his worldly delusion evaporated into nothingness, and his vital breath departed his mortal frame!
Through this ingenious, compassionate shock, Shivshankar Bhatt shattered the dying devotee&rsquo;s worldly infatuation, liberating his soul from the snare of earthly attachment at the final threshold of transition.
- From the discourses of Sadguru Brahmachari Aksharanand Swami&hellip;
