Goto Naviagation&#x1f447; English Joita Khachar&rsquo;s Golden Necklace in Kariyani, Vasta Khachar&rsquo;s Belated Realization, and the Grand Annakut Festival On one occasion in Kariyani, Bhagwan Swaminarayan was seated upon a cot in the inner courtyard of His staunch devotee, Darbar Vasta Khachar. Several visiting devotees lovingly offered a basket of ripe, fragrant mangoes at the Lord&rsquo;s holy feet.
Seated nearby was Vasta Khachar&rsquo;s young son, Joita Khachar, wearing an exquisite, heavy gold necklace (utari) around his neck and a shimmering gold-embroidered turban ornament (navgharu) upon his head. Smiling affectionately, Shreeji Maharaj extended a mango toward the child: &ldquo;Joita! Take this sweet mango.&rdquo;
The innocent boy scrambled onto his knees and held onto the wooden post of Maharaj&rsquo;s cot. Maharaj playfully bargained: &ldquo;If you surrender your gold necklace to Us, We shall give you this mango!&rdquo;
Without a second&rsquo;s hesitation, the young boy unclasped the precious gold necklace and placed it into Shreeji Maharaj&rsquo;s open palms, gleefully accepting the mango. Holding up a second golden fruit, Maharaj tested him again: &ldquo;Joita! Would you like another mango? Hand over that glittering turban ornament on your head!&rdquo; The child cheerfully took off the golden headdress, surrendered it to Maharaj, and seized the second mango.
Turning to the gathered assembly, Shreeji Maharaj extracted a profound spiritual truth from this innocent exchange: &ldquo;Behold, O sants and devotees! This is an innocent, naive child. Does he understand that his gold necklace and turban ornament could purchase entire cartloads of mangoes? Yet, captivated by the transient taste of a single fruit, he willingly surrendered invaluable gold! In precisely the same manner, the worldly jiva behaves like an ignorant child. Abandoning the Supreme Lord—the eternal fountainhead of divine bliss—the soul wanders aimlessly chasing perishable sensory pleasures. The jiva fails to realize that complete surrender unto God bestows ultimate fulfillment and permanently extinguishes all three types of worldly afflictions. Therefore, true seekers must never, like naive children, barter away the eternal treasure of the Supreme Lord for trivial, fleeting worldly enjoyments!&rdquo; (This historic sanctified necklace is preserved for darshan in the sacred treasury of Vadtal Mandir to this day).
On another occasion, Shreeji Maharaj stayed in Kariyani while en route to a grand spiritual assembly in Vadtal. At that time, Vasta Khachar was actively constructing a new residential home. Shreeji Maharaj urged him: &ldquo;Vasta Khachar! Leave the construction and accompany Us to Vadtal for the samaiya.&rdquo;
Entangled in domestic preoccupations, Vasta Khachar replied: &ldquo;O Maharaj! The house stands incomplete; the walls are raised and bullock carts have gone to Bhavnagar to fetch timber. Let me oversee this construction, and I shall join You shortly.&rdquo;
Maharaj cautioned him prophetically: &ldquo;You will not be able to come later; therefore, proceed with Us now.&rdquo; Yet Vasta Khachar persisted in his plan.
Shortly after Maharaj departed with the pilgrim caravan, a torrential unseasonal storm battered the region. The half-built earthen walls collapsed in ruins, paralyzing the work, and Vasta Khachar was utterly unable to travel to Vadtal. When Shreeji Maharaj returned to Kariyani after the festival, a remorseful Vasta Khachar fell at His feet: &ldquo;O Lord! Prioritizing mundane worldly construction over Your divine command was a grievous folly! I have forfeited the invaluable spiritual fellowship of the sants. Pray forgive my mistake and host a grand festival right here in Kariyani by summoning sants and devotees from all regions!&rdquo;
Pleased by his genuine repentance, Shreeji Maharaj dispatched letters across Gujarat and Kathiyawad, hosting a magnificent Annakut festival in Kariyani. In this sanctified town, Maharaj excavated the sacred Moksh-koop well, resided in the divine Akshar Ordi, and delivered twelve profound discourses recorded in the Vachanamrut.
- From &lsquo;Shree Harililamrutam&rsquo;, Kalash 6, Vishram 26&hellip;
