Goto Naviagation&#x1f447; English Muktaraj Mancha Khachar of Kariyani: Lifelong Celibacy, Renunciation upon the Letter from Bhadra, and Ascension as Achintyanand Swami Muktaraj Mancha Khachar of Kariyani was an extraordinarily cultured, spiritually zealous devotee who enjoyed the intimate grace of Bhagwan Swaminarayan. Though living amidst the worldly responsibilities of a Kathi chieftain, he remained utterly detached like a lotus flower untouched by water, dedicating his body, mind, and estate to the divine service of Shreeji Maharaj.
On one occasion, Mancha Khachar returned home on horseback from an outlying village, only to find confectioners distributing sugar crystals and drummers beating celebration drums in front of his residential courtyard. Dismounting, he inquired: &ldquo;What is this celebration for? Why are drums beating and sweets being distributed?&rdquo;
A merchant stepped forward with congratulations: &ldquo;Mancha Bapu! Your maternal uncle&rsquo;s daughter has been betrothed to you; we are celebrating your wedding alliance!&rdquo;
Hearing this, Mancha Khachar shuddered in moral indignation. He firmly rebuked them: &ldquo;A maternal uncle&rsquo;s daughter is my own sister! In fact, every woman in this entire world is like a revered mother or sister to me!&rdquo; With resolute determination, he dismissed the drummers and sent the platter of celebratory sugar back. Throughout his entire life, he upheld unblemished celibacy with ironclad integrity.
In the annals of the Swaminarayan fellowship, a historic event occurred when, upon receiving a single urgent letter from Shreeji Maharaj, eighteen prominent, wealthy householders instantly abandoned family, wealth, and status, donned saffron robes, and walked to Bhuj to meet the Lord. Foremost among those eighteen spiritual giants was Mancha Khachar of Kariyani!
When Virdas Patel arrived in Kariyani carrying Shreeji Maharaj&rsquo;s letter from Bhadra, Mancha Khachar was actively overseeing the excavation of a massive twelve-pulley agricultural well on his farm. Virdas called out: &ldquo;Bapu! An urgent letter has arrived from Maharaj!&rdquo;
Mancha Khachar instructed: &ldquo;Read it immediately.&rdquo;
The letter contained the famous unsparing injunction: &lsquo;If you are at the village gate, do not enter the village; if you are in the village, do not proceed to your house; and if you are at the threshold of your house, do not step inside. Upon reading this letter, depart immediately!&rsquo;
Hearing this unsparing command, Mancha Khachar asked without the slightest hesitation: &ldquo;Virdas! Did you bring red geru ochre dye with you?&rdquo;
Virdas replied: &ldquo;No, Bapu.&rdquo;
Mancha Khachar promptly sent for ochre clay from a local potter, dyed his clothes saffron right there in the fields, and set out on foot toward Kutch without visiting his mansion or bidding farewell to his kinsmen!
Remaining a lifelong celibate, Mancha Khachar renounced all worldly claims, leaving the management of his estates to his pious nephew, Vasta Khachar. During the catastrophic nationwide famine of Samvat 1869, Mancha Khachar and Vasta Khachar hosted hundreds of starving paramhansas in Kariyani for three full months, providing continuous food, clothing, and medicine. In Samvat 1877, when Shreeji Maharaj camped in Kariyani for three consecutive months, He delivered the twelve profound philosophical discourses preserved in the Vachanamrut within their court.
Subsequently, Shreeji Maharaj formally initiated Mancha Khachar as Sadguru Achintyanand Swami, commissioning him to preach across various provinces. Achintyanand Swami shed his mortal coil in Baman gam during Shreeji Maharaj&rsquo;s lifetime, entering eternal Akshardham. Upon receiving the news, Vasta Khachar went to Gadhada to invite Shreeji Maharaj and the entire congregation to Kariyani, hosting an opulent feast featuring traditional sata sweets in sacred commemoration of his liberated uncle.
- From &lsquo;Shree Harililamrutam&rsquo;, Kalash 7, Vishram 62&hellip;
