Goto Naviagation&#x1f447; English Mancha Khachar and Bhoja Charan&rsquo;s Quest for God, Excavation of Kariyani Pond, and the Historic First Journey to Gadhpur In the historic village of Kariyani near Sarangpur, the Kathi chieftain Darbar Mancha Khachar was a spiritually enlightened soul. Practicing strict lifelong celibacy and moral purity from his youth, he had long searched for an enlightened guru. Upon meeting Sadguru Ramanand Swami, he became his devoted disciple. Yet, an unquenchable spiritual thirst burned within his heart: &lsquo;If only I could attain the direct personal refuge of the manifest Supreme Godhead, my soul would attain ultimate liberation in this very life!&rsquo;
One afternoon, sitting in his courtyard alongside his trusted childhood friend Bhoja Charan, Mancha Khachar spoke earnestly: &ldquo;Bhoja! Either you set forth to find the manifest Lord, or I must go myself!&rdquo;
Bhoja Charan replied with affectionate loyalty: &ldquo;Bapu! You are the ruler of this estate and have great public responsibilities; let me go on this holy quest in your stead.&rdquo;
Embarking with a pure and earnest heart, Bhoja Charan traversed Kathiyawad. Journeying past Dwarka and Prabhas Patan, he heard startling rumors along the coastal belt: a peerless divine incarnation had arrived in the Loj-Mangrol region, effortlessly inducing profound meditative trances (nirvikalpa samadhi) in ordinary souls through a mere divine glance! Hurrying to Loj, Bhoja Charan beheld Bhagwan Swaminarayan in person. Seeing seekers entering superconscious realms and witnessing hardened sinners undergoing miraculous transformations of character, Bhoja Charan attained absolute conviction that this youth was the manifest Supreme Being.
Overjoyed, he raced back to Kariyani. Spotting his friend approaching with a beaming countenance, Mancha Khachar called out from the village square: &ldquo;What news, Bhoja? Why does your face shine with such radiant bliss?&rdquo;
Bhoja Charan declared with ecstatic certainty: &ldquo;Bapu! I have discovered the manifest Supreme Lord!&rdquo;
Mancha Khachar inquired with breathless anticipation: &ldquo;Where does this divine Lord dwell at this moment?&rdquo;
Bhoja Charan replied: &ldquo;He is currently residing in Loj near Mangrol port. Thousands are blessed with instant samadhi by His mere glance, beholding divine visions of the supreme abode!&rdquo;
Without losing a single moment, Mancha Khachar departed for Loj. Meeting Shreeji Maharaj in Mangrol, the very first sight of the Lord cast Mancha Khachar into an instantaneous, deep samadhi wherein he beheld the supreme splendor of Akshardham. Awakening in tears of devotional ecstasy, he prostrated at Shreeji Maharaj&rsquo;s feet, recognizing Him as the sovereign Lord. Sadguru Ramanand Swami confirmed his divine conviction before passing the spiritual leadership to Sahajanand Swami.
Accompanying the Lord during the festivals at Sardhar, Mancha Khachar pleaded with Shreeji Maharaj to visit his home in Kariyani. When the Lord arrived, Mancha Khachar and his nephew Vasta Khachar organized lavish hospitality for the sants and held a magnificent Kartiki Purnima assembly.
During this period, Shreeji Maharaj initiated the excavation of a massive community reservoir on the northern edge of Kariyani. Thousands of devotees and ascetics converged from across Saurashtra, working day and night with immense devotion. Shreeji Maharaj sat on an elevated platform overseeing the work, warmly embracing devotees as they carried baskets of earth. To sustain the workers, fifteen maunds of pearl millet soaked in water with five maunds of sesame seeds were prepared each evening, and every morning Shreeji Maharaj personally distributed sanctified handfuls into each laborer&rsquo;s open palms. Once filled, Maharaj engaged in joyous water sports with the sants. The entire financial expenditure was borne by Mancha Khachar and Vasta Khachar.
Following the Vasant Panchami celebrations, Shreeji Maharaj departed from Kariyani on Maha Sud 11, Samvat 1861, arriving in Gadhada for the very first time in history. There, captivated by the pure devotion of Ebhal Bapu, Jayaba, Lalitaba, and the four-year-old child-devotee Dada Khachar, the Lord made Gadhada His permanent earthly residence.
