Goto Naviagation&#x1f447; English Darbar Abhaysinhji&rsquo;s Meditation in Dhoraji, Dehabhai Charan&rsquo;s Quest for Peace, and Association with Gunatitanand Swami On one occasion, the devout chieftain of Lodhika, Darbar Abhaysinhji, traveled to Junagadh to seek the sacred darshan of Shree Radharaman Dev and bask in the spiritual company of Sadguru Gunatitanand Swami. After spending several blissful days absorbing scriptural discourses, he sought Swami&rsquo;s blessings and set out on horseback toward Lodhika.
En route, under the midday sun, he reached the rural outskirts of Dhoraji village and paused by an agricultural orchard along the road. Observing the cooling shade of lush trees and a farmer operating a water-pulley (kosh) at a well, Darbar Abhaysinhji greeted the farmer with &lsquo;Jai Swaminarayan&rsquo;, watered his horse, and tethered it under the canopy of trees. Washing his hands and feet, he filtered water through a clean cloth to drink. Then, sitting in the full lotus posture (padmasana) upon the grass, he closed his eyes and became deeply absorbed in meditating upon the divine form of Shreeji Maharaj.
Simultaneously, a noble spiritual seeker from Marwad in Rajasthan named Dehabhai Charan arrived at the same well. Weary of the transience and futility of worldly life, Dehabhai had developed intense spiritual detachment (vairagya). Believing that Mount Girnar in Saurashtra was the sacred sanctuary of realized masters (siddhas), he had traveled on foot all the way from Marwad to find an enlightened guru whose commands he could follow to achieve spiritual liberation. He had climbed Girnar, wandering among numerous anchorites, yogis, and ascetics; yet, none had imparted genuine spiritual peace to his restless soul. Deeply dejected, he was walking back toward Marwad when, seeking rest, he entered the very same farm in Dhoraji.
Greeting the peasant with &lsquo;Ram Ram&rsquo;, Dehabhai refreshed himself and sat beneath the tree. Turning around, his eyes fell upon the meditating form of Darbar Abhaysinhji. Dehabhai stared in awe. The broad, radiant forehead marked with the blazing saffron-and-sandalwood tilak-chandlo, the double-stranded sacred tulsi beads around the neck, the serene countenance, and the profound stillness of his meditative absorption cast an immediate, miraculous wave of sublime tranquility over Dehabhai&rsquo;s heart! He marveled in astonishment: &lsquo;How astonishing! The deep inner peace I could not find among the celebrated ascetics of Mount Girnar has saturated my entire soul by merely looking upon this meditating householder!&rsquo;
When Darbar Abhaysinhji opened his eyes, he noticed Dehabhai watching him with folded hands and greeted him: &ldquo;Jai Swaminarayan!&rdquo;
Dehabhai inquired with earnest curiosity: &ldquo;Noble Sir! From which kingdom do you hail? And from what divine fountainhead does this profound spiritual serenity radiating from your countenance flow?&rdquo;
Darbar Abhaysinhji smiled humbly: &ldquo;I am Darbar Abhaysinhji, the chieftain of Lodhika, and an ordinary householder devotee of Bhagwan Shree Swaminarayan. I am no ascetic renunciant; yet, the supreme spiritual master, Sadguru Gunatitanand Swami, currently presides over the Junagadh mandir. It is through his divine association and divine discourses that this unbroken inner peace abides within our hearts!&rdquo;
Tears welled in Dehabhai&rsquo;s eyes as he exclaimed: &ldquo;Bapu! I scoured Mount Girnar in vain searching for peace, but your presence has brought calmness to my heart! Pray send me to this divine master in Junagadh!&rdquo;
Darbar Abhaysinhji gladly penned a warm letter of recommendation to Gunatitanand Swami. Armed with the letter, Dehabhai reached Junagadh the next morning, offered prostrations at the feet of Gunatitanand Swami, and handed him the message.
Reading the letter, Gunatitanand Swami welcomed him with overflowing affection: &ldquo;Brother! This is the Lord&rsquo;s own divine court. The gates of this temple are forever open to sincere seekers of God. Consider this your own home; listen to spiritual discourses, serve the deities, and engage in joyful devotion!&rdquo;
The motherly warmth of Swami&rsquo;s words and presence instantly dissolved all of Dehabhai&rsquo;s lingering fatigue and worldly distress. Immersing himself in humble temple service, he requested monastic initiation a year later. Gunatitanand Swami had him formally ordained by the Acharya Maharaj, giving him the monastic name Sadguru Shrikrishnapriyadasji. He spent the remainder of his life in Junagadh, devotedly serving Shree Radharaman Dev and Gunatitanand Swami.
- From &lsquo;Sorathi Santo&rsquo;&hellip;
