Goto Naviagation&#x1f447; English Shreeji Maharaj Disguised as a Policeman Guards Shamdas Sheth&rsquo;s Shop in Savda In the town of Savda, there lived a prosperous and highly respected merchant named Shamdas Sheth, who had embraced Satsang through the divine association of the paramhansas. He was a devout, ekantik devotee of Bhagwan Shree Swaminarayan. Whenever traveling sadhus visited Savda, Shamdas lovingly welcomed them into his home, hosted them with great reverence, and sponsored sumptuous feasts (rasoi) to feed them with deep devotion.
Shamdas owned a prominent trading shop situated in the central market of Savda. One evening, after concluding a day of heavy trading, he inadvertently forgot to latch the main wooden doors. In his distraction, he simply snapped the iron padlock through the outer ring without sliding the secure bolt into place, leaving the shop effectively unlocked. Inside lay the entire day&rsquo;s cash collection, precious commodities, and valuable merchandise. Unaware of his oversight, Shamdas returned home and peacefully retired for the night.
However, the Lord, being ever-protective of His surrender-seeking devotees, could not let His servant suffer ruin. Assuming the form of an imposing policeman dressed in uniform and armed with a loaded rifle, Bhaktavatsal Shreeji Maharaj paced diligently outside Shamdas&rsquo;s shop, keeping strict vigil throughout the cold night. Directly opposite the shop was a confectioner&rsquo;s (kandoi) bakery, where the sweetmaker was working through the night to prepare confections for an upcoming wedding. Seeing the armed guard patrolling steadily before Shamdas&rsquo;s establishment, the confectioner thought to himself: &ldquo;Shamdas Sheth must have stored some nobleman&rsquo;s heavy treasury inside his shop tonight, which is why an armed guard has been hired to patrol all night long.&rdquo;
At four o&rsquo;clock in the morning, having guarded the store safely through the night, Shreeji Maharaj walked to Shamdas&rsquo;s residence and called out from the courtyard: &ldquo;Shamdas..! O Shamdas..! You locked your shop without fastening the bolt, forcing Us to stand guard all night long!&rdquo;
Because Shamdas was fast asleep, he did not immediately hear the call. However, his elderly mother, resting on a cot in the veranda, heard the divine, authoritative voice. She hurried inside and shook her son awake: &ldquo;Shamdas! Wake up at once! A divine personage resembling Shreeji Maharaj just stood outside our door, declaring that you left your shop unlatched and that He had to keep guard the entire night!&rdquo;
Startled, Shamdas sprang out of bed and rushed straight to the marketplace. To his horror, he found the doors unfastened with the padlock dangling loosely. At that moment, the sweetmaker approached him and exclaimed: &ldquo;Shamdas Sheth! Was a royal treasure deposited in your shop yesterday? A policeman with a loaded rifle stood strict sentry at your doorway throughout the entire night!&rdquo;
Tears of overwhelmed gratitude cascaded down Shamdas&rsquo;s cheeks. He replied with profound devotion: &ldquo;Brother, no royal treasure was stored here. In my negligence, I had walked away leaving the door unbolted. To protect His humble servant from ruin, my beloved Ishtadev, Bhagwan Shree Swaminarayan Himself, stayed awake through the dark night and stood guard with a rifle! Just moments ago, He came to my home to awaken me. How matchless is His compassion for His devotees!&rdquo;
Hearing this miraculous account, the sweetmaker&rsquo;s heart was profoundly touched. Conviction in the divinity of Bhagwan Swaminarayan dawned within him, and he immediately embraced Satsang. For the remainder of his life, he testified to everyone with unwavering conviction: &ldquo;Swaminarayan is truly the Supreme Lord! I have beheld Him with my very own eyes standing guard through the night to protect His devotee!&rdquo;
- From the divine discourses of Sadguru Brahmachari Shree Aksharanand Swami&hellip;
