Goto Naviagation&#x1f447; English The Rudri-Chaurasi Festival at Bhimnath Mahadev, Hira Kothari&rsquo;s Trial, and Shreehari&rsquo;s Divine Rescue as the Merchant Journeying through Taldi village, Bhagwan Shreeji Maharaj arrived at the ancient sacred shrine of Bhimnath Mahadev, accompanied by spirited Kathi chieftains, the devoted merchant Bhaga Sheth, His scrupulous treasurer Hira Kothari, and an assembly of disciples. Entering the temple precincts, Shreeji Maharaj summoned the head priest (Bavaji) and announced: &ldquo;Bavaji! Today We wish to offer a grand feast (thal) worth one hundred and five rupees to Lord Shiva!&rdquo;
Turning to His treasurer, Maharaj commanded: &ldquo;Kothari! Produce the money at once!&rdquo;
Standing right beside Kothari, the noble merchant Bhaga Sheth instantly drew a purse from his coat and placed one hundred and five silver coins into the priest&rsquo;s hands. The priest reverently consecrated the offering before the Shiva Lingam.
Immediately following this, Shreeji Maharaj declared: &ldquo;Bavaji! We now desire to sponsor an elaborate Rudri recitation and host a grand feast (chaurasi) for one hundred and eight scholarly Brahmins. Summon all ceremonial ingredients and supplies immediately!&rdquo;
Hearing this unexpected order, Hira Kothari&rsquo;s face turned as grim and pale as a black blanket! Meanwhile, the ecstatic Mahant summoned his four principal disciples: &ldquo;Bhairavnath, Teerathnath, Gomtinath, Ganganath! Swaminarayan wishes to perform Rudri and feed one hundred Brahmins; procure all provisions and organize the grand banquet at once!&rdquo;
Watching the ascetics rush to execute the orders, Hira Kothari edged close to Maharaj and whispered anxiously: &ldquo;Alas, Maharaj! Before committing to such enormous expenditures, pray consider our funds! I possess a meager twenty-five rupees in my treasury; please exercise restraint!&rdquo;
Overhearing the treasurer&rsquo;s cautious whisper, the fiery Kathi chieftains flared up with annoyance: &ldquo;Look at this merchant counting pennies! How absurd—the Master spends with royal generosity, yet the servant writhes in agony!&rdquo;
The witty chieftain Sura Khachar strode to Maharaj&rsquo;s side and urged: &ldquo;Maharaj! Never mind his penny-pinching; our honor must never be compromised! Spend with boundless freedom! If debts are incurred, those destined to pay will pay; if a trench is dug, it will surely be filled!&rdquo;
Mulji Brahmachari chimed in: &ldquo;Indeed, Sura Khachar speaks the truth! Is this how the treasurer of God behaves? Such a miserly nature is intolerable. If a treasurer obstructs his Master&rsquo;s noble religious causes, what remains of the Master&rsquo;s reputation?&rdquo;
Shreeji Maharaj listened with an amused smile playing on His lips. Pausing on the temple steps, He turned to the chieftains and gently reprimanded them: &ldquo;Chieftains! Brahmachari! Your sentiments may sound bold, but if I dismiss Hira Kothari today, do any of you know another treasurer so immaculate, honest, and meticulous down to the last penny?&rdquo;
Sura Khachar retorted: &ldquo;Maharaj, a cart stuck in an alley does not remain stuck forever! If not him, another will easily be found.&rdquo;
Maharaj frowned sternly and rebuked Sura Khachar: &ldquo;Enough, Sura Khachar! Speak no further! Never entertain such thoughts. As long as We are guarded by such an honest and pure-hearted soul, neither Kal (death) nor Maya (illusion) can dare approach him!&rdquo;
Bhaga Sheth promptly stepped forward and sponsored the costs of the Rudri ritual and the Brahmin feast. By afternoon, the rites concluded and the Brahmins departed fully satisfied. However, additional logistical expenses and ritual dakshina mounted to a staggering ten thousand rupees! Without warning, Shreeji Maharaj suddenly set out toward Bhavnagar with His entourage, intentionally leaving Hira Kothari behind at Bhimnath to settle the entire outstanding balance!
Hira Kothari fell into sheer panic: &lsquo;Good heavens! In this strange, unfamiliar territory, where shall I ever procure ten thousand rupees? Maharaj incurred this enormous bill and left me stranded!&rsquo; Overwhelmed with anxiety, he collapsed beneath a roadside tree, weeping in helpless desperation.
Presently, a magnificent royal carriage (baggi) rolled up, kicking up dust, and halted beside him. An extraordinarily radiant, opulent merchant stepped out and inquired politely: &ldquo;Brother! We are journeying to Bhimnath Mahadev; how far is the temple from here?&rdquo;
Hira Kothari replied in a weak, trembling voice: &ldquo;Behold the temple spire fluttering just ahead; it is merely a few furlongs away.&rdquo;
Observing his distress, the merchant asked with deep concern: &ldquo;Why do you speak with such profound sorrow? Are you unwell, or are you ensnared in some terrible predicament?&rdquo;
Unable to contain his grief, Hira Kothari sobbed: &ldquo;Noble Sir! My calamity knows no bounds! I am a merchant from Gadhada, serving as treasurer to Bhagwan Swaminarayan. The Lord visited Bhimnath, organized Rudri and fed hundreds of Brahmins. Although I warned Him I held only twenty-five rupees, He paid no heed, ran up a debt of ten thousand rupees, and departed for Bhavnagar, leaving me stranded! In this alien region, who will ever lend ten thousand rupees to an unknown stranger? I am utterly ruined!&rdquo;
The merchant smiled warmly and consoled him: &ldquo;Revered merchant! Centuries ago, when devotee Narasinh Mehta wrote a promissory note (hundi) on Dwarka, who knew him there? You are a merchant&rsquo;s son, and we too are merchants! We shall gladly advance you the ten thousand rupees. Cast away your sorrow, write us a promissory note, and in a few days we will come to Gadhada to collect our money.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Indeed, you are my living God!&rdquo; Hira wept in gratitude. He wrote out the promissory note, and the merchant handed him ten bags, each packed with a thousand silver coins (totaling ten thousand rupees), before driving off into the distance.
Rushing back to the temple, Hira Kothari counted out the full amount to the Mahant, secured an official signed receipt, and hurried through the night until he reached Bhavnagar.
Shreeji Maharaj welcomed him with a knowing smile: &ldquo;Kothari! Have you settled everything satisfactorily?&rdquo;
Hira displayed the parchment triumphantly: &ldquo;Yes, Maharaj! I settled the entire ten-thousand rupee bill with the Mahant and secured this receipt!&rdquo;
Maharaj innocently queried: &ldquo;But you possessed no money; whence did you obtain such a fortune?&rdquo;
Hira answered: &ldquo;Maharaj! A benevolent, wealthy merchant arrived in a carriage and advanced the entire sum without hesitation. I issued him a hundi payable at Gadhada, and he promised to visit soon to collect.&rdquo;
Retiring to bed, Hira Kothari marveled in bewilderment: who was that divine stranger who had trusted an unknown traveler with a fortune without security? Little did he fathom that to preserve the dignity of His loyal, honest servant, Shreeji Maharaj Himself had manifested as the legendary merchant Shamalsha Sheth to save him!
