Goto Naviagation&#x1f447; English The Persecution of Shrimali Brahmin Janbai of Trapaj, Her Refuge in Gadhpur, and Her Ascent to Akshardham In the village of Trapaj within the Talaja taluka of Bhavnagar district, there lived a spiritually elevated and detached woman of the Shrimali Brahmin caste named Janbai. From the moment of her birth, she manifested the exalted virtues of a realized soul carrying profound spiritual detachment (vairagya) from past lives. In her tender childhood, a Swaminarayan temple stood directly adjacent to her parental home. Listening constantly to the inspiring scriptural discourses of traveling sadhus, her mind became firmly anchored in moral disciplines, righteous virtues, and divine worship. Before she reached adulthood, the seeds of supreme devotion had firmly blossomed in her heart.
However, upon entering her marital home after marriage, her spiritual path was confronted by relentless tribulations. Her in-laws were spiritually ignorant, worldly, and entirely hostile toward Satsang. The household was governed by the tyrannical matriarchy of her harsh mother-in-law. On one morning, while performing her daily meditation and worship of the Lord, Janbai was slightly delayed in attending to the household chores. Finding the sweeping and kitchen chores unattended, her mother-in-law unloosed a tongue like a sharpened sword: &ldquo;Hey there, Janbai bride! You have become an obsessive devotee of religion! Fix your mind on household drudgery first; that alone is a married woman&rsquo;s true religion! Worshiping stone idols will never feed your stomach! Pay heed to domestic duties and caste customs, or else when children are born, survival will become a torment! Right now I stand here to run this household; once I am gone, you will find life utterly unlivable!&rdquo;
Janbai replied with gentle serenity: &ldquo;Respected Mother! When a soul places wholehearted trust in the Supreme Almighty, what worry remains of this transient world? Did illustrious saints like Narasinh Mehta and Mirabai ever despair over material anxieties? Yet did not God miraculously provide for all their worldly necessities?&rdquo;
Hearing this tranquil reply, the mother-in-law sneered venomously: &ldquo;Oho! Look at the great enlightened philosopher (Brahmagnani) she has become! How dare you speak back to me with such insolence! Today my counsel sounds bitter to you, but mark my words, you will weep tears of blood when life shatters you!&rdquo;
Janbai could seek no refuge in her parental home either, where her impoverished elderly mother barely eked out a solitary sustenance from daily alms. In her marital home, her husband was mentally sluggish and meek, leaving the abusive mother-in-law with unchecked dominion. Forbidden from praying, meditating, or even chanting the Lord&rsquo;s name, Janbai&rsquo;s soul fell into excruciating despair. Driven to the brink of insanity by constant mental and physical torment, she contemplated ending her life.
One night, weeping bitterly in her bedchamber, she cried out to Shreehari: &ldquo;O Lord! My endurance is exhausted. Suicide is indeed a grave sin; yet to escape this ceaseless persecution, I am driven to that tragic precipice!&rdquo;
At that very moment, Bhagwan Shreeji Maharaj manifested before her in a radiant, divine vision! Grasping her trembling hand, Maharaj said: &ldquo;Janbai! Despair not! The fruits of past karmas must indeed be endured, but suicide is an unpardonable mistake. Your spiritual path is long and glorious. To fulfill the true purpose of your soul, abandon this misery and come dwell with Us in Gadhpur!&rdquo;
Her path illuminated by the Lord&rsquo;s command, Janbai quietly slipped out of the village that very night and journeyed to Gadhpur. Arriving at the royal court of King Dada Khachar, she joined the holy order of female renunciants (sankhyayogis) under the loving guardianship of Jivuba and Laduba. Immersed in humble service, she basked in the serene bliss of continuous devotion.
However, worldly society never tolerates a captive escaping its grasp. Her furious in-laws gathered influential community elders of the Brahmin caste and launched a search. Learning that Janbai was residing at the Swaminarayan compound in Gadhada, the belligerent in-laws and caste leaders marched into the royal courtyard, confronting Shreeji Maharaj insolently: &ldquo;Swaminarayan! You have harbored our son&rsquo;s wedded wife! We have come to claim her; surrender her to us immediately!&rdquo;
To test her resolve before the entire assembly, Shreeji Maharaj summoned Janbai and addressed her in a stern, detached voice: &ldquo;Janbai! Your in-laws have arrived to take you back; you must accompany them home.&rdquo;
Hearing these cold words from the very Lord whom she regarded as her sole saviour, Janbai felt as if struck by a thunderbolt! She stood motionless, petrified in utter shock. A torrent of agony flooded her thoughts: &lsquo;Alas! What horrific sins have I committed in past lives! Neither in my father&rsquo;s house nor in my husband&rsquo;s home was there any peace. When at last I found sanctuary at the feet of God to liberate my soul, God Himself casts me out! Where shall I turn now? There is no one in this entire universe to call my own!&rsquo;
Overwhelmed by heartbreak and despair, her senses gave way, and she collapsed unconscious upon the stone floor!
Witnessing her fall, Shreeji Maharaj immediately stepped down from His seat, rushed to her side, and gently revived her: &ldquo;Janbai! Dispel all grief and fear! Everything shall unfold according to your innermost wish; have complete faith in Us. We know full well that no peace awaits you in that household. Open your heart freely and speak your true desire!&rdquo;
Fluttering her eyes open, Janbai whispered in faint, trembling tones: &ldquo;O Supreme Lord! My frail mortal frame can bear the tortures of this worldly existence no longer. Just as a solitary withered tree in the wild dries up and falls, my worldly life is extinguished. I harbor no desire except the eternal devotion of Your lotus feet. I beseech You, grant me liberation from this mortal sphere and summon me into Your divine Akshardham!&rdquo;
Knowing her profound detachment, Maharaj comforted her: &ldquo;Absorb your mind entirely in meditating upon God; your sacred resolve shall be granted this very day!&rdquo;
Anchoring her absolute faith in the Lord&rsquo;s divine promise, Janbai closed her eyes, stilled every worldly thought, and immersed herself in the ceaseless chanting of the Swaminarayan Mahamantra. Within two hours, a transcendental spiritual fever enveloped her frame, severing the subtle cords linking consciousness to flesh. Beholding the Lord&rsquo;s enchanting divine form with her final mortal breath, she consciously cast aside her physical body, assumed an effulgent divine form, and ascended directly to Akshardham!
Her arrogant in-laws and the caste elders watched in sheer, dumbfounded horror! In an instant, Janbai&rsquo;s lifeless body lay still before them. Shreeji Maharaj turned to the stunned Brahmins and announced: &ldquo;Noble Brahmins! Behold the mortal remains of your daughter-in-law Janbai. Her liberated soul has departed into Our eternal abode of Akshardham; she desired to dwell in this miserable world no longer. Now, carry this earthly frame away and perform her funeral rites according to your caste traditions.&rdquo;
Deeply remorseful, ashamed, and grief-stricken, the in-laws carried Janbai&rsquo;s mortal remains to the banks of the sacred Ghela river, performed the last rites, and returned to Trapaj with lowered heads and empty hands.
- From Nari-Ratno Part - 1&hellip;
