Goto Naviagation&#x1f447; English Shreehari&rsquo;s Divine Pastime of Stealing Rotlis in Sarkapar: Muktanand Swami&rsquo;s Wit and the Vaniya&rsquo;s Deliverance During the blazing heat of summer, Supreme Lord Shreehari was travelling through the arid realm of Kutch accompanied by a large entourage of paramhansas. As the journey progressed, the holy company arrived at the outskirts of Sarkapar village. Beholding a tranquil lake shaded by ancient, sprawling banyan trees, Shreehari resolved to set up camp there. As the noon hour approached for the midday meal (thal), Shreehari said to Sadguru Muktanand Swami with a twinkle of divine mystery, &ldquo;Swami! Have all the sadhus bathe in the soothing waters of the lake and rest. Meanwhile, I shall personally go into the village with my begging sack (zoli) to seek alms.&rdquo; The enlightened saints knew well that beneath the playful surface of the Lord&rsquo;s words lay a profound purpose waiting to unfold.
Shreehari playfully disguised Himself as an itinerant mendicant. He donned an ochre ascetic robe (alfi), draped a white cotton cloth across His shoulder, and slung an ochre begging sack over His arm. His beard and locks were gently grown, and holding a small three-foot wooden staff in hand while treading in wooden clogs (chakhadi) that echoed with a rhythmic tap-tap, the Supreme Lord strolled into the bustling lanes of Sarkapar.
Halting outside several houses, Shreehari called out, &ldquo;Jai Sachchidanand&hellip; Narayan Hare&hellip; Bhikshannam Dehi&hellip;!&rdquo; Receiving no response from the first five homes, He arrived at the sixth doorway. Seeing the wooden courtyard gate ajar and a long verandah leading to the kitchen, Shreehari stepped inside and called softly, &ldquo;Narayan Hare&hellip; Sachchidanand Prabho&hellip;!&rdquo; The dwelling belonged to a prosperous local merchant (Vaniya). Inside the inner kitchen, the merchant&rsquo;s wife was busily engaged in rolling and roasting fresh wheat rotlis over the earthen hearth. So completely absorbed was her mind in her culinary duties that she failed to hear the Lord&rsquo;s call. Peeking through the door, Shreehari noticed a warm, towering stack of freshly prepared rotlis resting by the hearth. With a smile, the Lord thought to Himself, &lsquo;We have often dined on requested alms; today let Us relish the divine joy of stealing them!&rsquo;
Stepping noiselessly into the kitchen like a playful child, Shreehari gently lifted the entire stack of rotlis. At that precise instant, the woman lifted another roasted flatbread from the griddle to place it atop the pile, only to discover the entire stack had vanished! Glancing toward the sunlit courtyard, she saw a tall, ochre-clad ascetic briskly running out the gate with the warm bread tucked under His arm! In a flash of outrage, she snatched up her rolling pin and pursued Him, screaming at the top of her lungs, &ldquo;Run! Catch the thief! Thief! Thief&hellip;!&rdquo;
Her husband, the merchant, was sitting at his nearby shop. Hearing his wife&rsquo;s panicked cries, he rushed out. &ldquo;What happened?&rdquo; he yelled.
&ldquo;That wicked sadhu stole all my rotlis from the kitchen and defiled my cooking hearth!&rdquo; she shrieked. Instantly infuriated, the merchant hitched up his dhoti to his knees and dashed after Shreehari, shouting fiercely, &ldquo;Stop, thief! Stop!&rdquo; Several villagers joined the chase with clubs and cries. Shreehari sprinted swiftly into the lakeside encampment, blended quietly among the seated saints, pulled a shawl over His head, and began turning a rosary with immaculate composure!
The furious merchant and the mob barged into the peaceful assembly of ascetics, shouting violently, &ldquo;Drag out that rogue ascetic hiding under the shawl! Hand him over to us!&rdquo;
Sadguru Muktanand Swami, observing the muffled figure with the telltale stack of rotlis beneath the shawl, instantly grasped the Lord&rsquo;s playful divine scheme. Swami rose calmly, approached the fuming merchant with great serenity, and said gently, &ldquo;Peace, noble Sheth! The holy congregation of sadhus is your guest today. Pray, what grave transgression has provoked such fiery anger?&rdquo;
The crowd exclaimed, &ldquo;Your sadhu sneaked into the Sheth&rsquo;s private kitchen and stole his fresh rotlis!&rdquo;
Muktanand Swami chuckled warmly and responded with disarming humor, &ldquo;Oh! Is that all, noble Sheth? Alas, that particular sadhu is somewhat eccentric and simple-minded! Does our vast fellowship lack food? Tell us, how many rotlis did he take?&rdquo;
&ldquo;Five rotlis!&rdquo; the merchant snapped.
Muktanand Swami smiled soothingly, &ldquo;Consider it settled, Sheth! We shall give you five large sweet ladus from our sacred offerings. We rarely permit him to enter villages, for when his madness strikes, he even begins pelting stones! He frequently lands us in trouble. To atone for his folly, we gladly offer you one additional ladu—six in total.&rdquo;
Seeing the merchant hesitate out of ingrained commercial greed, Muktanand Swami added, &ldquo;If six seem inadequate, here, take eight ladus!&rdquo; Swami motioned to a devotee, handed eight rich, golden ladus into the merchant&rsquo;s hands, and patted him affectionately on the back, saying, &ldquo;Remember the illustrious charity of your ancestors, Vastupal and Tejpal. Anger ill becomes a scion of such benevolent lineage.&rdquo;
Pleased at having traded five flatbreads for eight sweet confections, the merchant smirked, &ldquo;Indeed, holy father! But if ascetics roam about filching bread, where will righteousness dwell?&rdquo; He walked away thoroughly self-satisfied with his profitable barter.
Returning to his shop, he presented the eight ladus to his wife with pride, saying, &ldquo;Look! I extracted eight delicious ladus in return for five rotlis—principal recovered with hefty interest! Their head priest is exceedingly courteous, but what can one say about that lunatic ascetic!&rdquo;
At that moment, Santokbai, a devout satsangi woman from Bhuj who was present in the market crowd, stepped forward and rebuked him: &ldquo;O blind fool! That ascetic was none other than the Supreme Lord Swaminarayan Himself! You failed to recognize the Lord of the cosmos who stood at your doorway! The entire divine company is camped at the village outskirts!&rdquo;
Hearing this, the merchant and his wife stood frozen, their faces drained of color. Santokbai continued, &ldquo;What dreadful folly! When the Supreme Divinity arrives at your outskirts, instead of offering devotional alms, you extorted sweetmeats from saints over five morsels of bread! You traded your spiritual salvation and honor for trivial gluttony!&rdquo;
Deep remorse pierced the merchant&rsquo;s heart. He resolved, &lsquo;I shall not consume a single grain of food until I have served and fed that holy assembly.&rsquo; He hurried to the lake, while remorseful villagers gathered cartloads of flour, ghee, sugar, and vegetables to prepare a lavish feast for the holy congregation.
Meanwhile, having consumed the extorted ladus upon returning home, the merchant suddenly experienced an agonizing burning sensation coursing through his limbs! He writhed on the floor in unbearable torment. His terrified wife pleaded, &ldquo;Behold the terrible fruit of usurping food meant for saints! The Lord is still by the lake—run at once, clasp His feet, and beg for mercy before your very life is extinguished!&rdquo;
Trembling and sobbing, the merchant and his wife rushed to the camp. The saints had just finished dining, and Shreeji Maharaj was seated regally in a divine assembly. Weeping bitterly, the merchant threw himself at Shreehari&rsquo;s lotus feet, pleading for forgiveness. The all-compassionate Lord smiled sweetly and cast an ocean of benevolent grace upon him. The burning agony vanished instantly. Overflowing with profound devotion, the merchant and his family accepted the sacred vows (Panch Vartman) and took lifelong refuge at the divine feet of Bhagwan Swaminarayan.
