Goto Naviagation&#x1f447; English At Ruda Patel&rsquo;s Farm in Samatra: Inexhaustible Rotlas from Amrutbai&rsquo;s Basket and Shreehari Resting on Wheat Straw While touring the arid expanse of Kutch alongside a multitude of paramhansas, Supreme Lord Shreehari arrived at the village of Samatra. Inquiring about His intimate devotee Ruda Patel, the messenger informed Him, &ldquo;Maharaj! Ruda Patel is currently working at his agricultural farm outside the village.&rdquo; Hearing this, Shreeji Maharaj led the holy congregation directly to the lush farmland. Beholding the cool, sprawling canopy of an ancient banyan tree beside a deep well, Shreehari declared, &ldquo;We shall pitch our camp right here today!&rdquo;
As the midday sun beat down upon the countryside, Ruda Patel&rsquo;s devoted wife, Amrutbai, arrived at the farm carrying a large woven basket on her head containing the lunch (bhat) prepared for her husband and the day-laborers. Cresting the boundary ridge of the field, her eyes fell upon the well, where her beloved Lord Shreehari and a vast gathering of ochre-robed ascetics were peacefully resting. A wave of overwhelming devotional ecstasy surged through her heart! Rushing beneath the shade of the banyan tree, she set down the basket, spread the corner of her sari, and offered prostrations (panchang pranam) at the Lord&rsquo;s lotus feet, exclaiming with a choked voice:
&ldquo;O merciful Lord! You have become so rare to behold! For days on end, my heart was incessantly chanting Your holy name. Today You have graced and sanctified our humble farm! The noon hour has arrived; let me immediately light an earthen hearth right here and bake fresh rotlas. You must dine on our offerings today!&rdquo;
Perceiving Amrutbai&rsquo;s pure, heartfelt devotion, Shreehari smiled affectionately and said, &ldquo;Amrutbai! You already have freshly cooked rotlas in your lunch basket! Why kindle another fire in this blistering heat? Serve us from what you have already brought!&rdquo;
Amrutbai replied with anxiety, &ldquo;O Lord! There are only two or three flatbreads meant solely for Patel and the laborers. How could such a meager portion suffice for this vast congregation of ascetics? It will take me but a few moments to dispatch Patel to fetch more flour from our home! Today You have become our divine guest; when shall we ever obtain such an auspicious fortune again?&rdquo;
Witnessing her overflowing sincerity, Shreehari was exceedingly pleased. Walking over to the lunch basket, the Lord untied the wrapping cloth and lifted out two millet rotlas. Breaking them with His divine hands into small morsels, Shreehari began distributing the pieces to the entire company of sadhus and devotees, accompanied by cool buttermilk and spicy pickle. Amrutbai watched in breathless awe. Just as Lord Krishna had miraculously satisfied Sage Durvasa and his ten thousand disciples in the forest with a single vegetable leaf from Draupadi&rsquo;s Akshay Patra, Shreehari multiplied the two rotlas through His divine potency, feeding every saint and devotee until all were thoroughly satisfied! Amazingly, when the distribution concluded, the rotlas, buttermilk, and pickle in Amrutbai&rsquo;s basket remained entirely undiminished—as though not a single crumb had been touched!
Shreehari looked upon Amrutbai with an ocean of divine affection and said, &ldquo;Behold, Amrutbai! We have fulfilled your cherished desire. We have relished your offerings, and our hunger has been wholly satisfied!&rdquo;
Meanwhile, Ruda Patel and his laborers, who had been working in a distant corner of the field, approached the well for lunch. Seeing Shreeji Maharaj conversing pleasantly with Amrutbai under the banyan tree, Ruda Patel was overcome with unspeakable joy. Rushing forward, he fell at the Lord&rsquo;s feet in continuous prostrations. Shreehari lovingly raised His devotee and embraced him tightly.
Ruda Patel said urgently to his wife, &ldquo;Quickly prepare a fire; I shall run home to fetch provisions to prepare a grand feast for Maharaj and the sadhus!&rdquo;
Before Amrutbai could reply, Shreehari laughed softly and said, &ldquo;Ruda Patel! Your wife has already fed us sumptuously from your lunch basket! We are completely satisfied. Now you and your laborers sit down and dine, for you are weary and hungry!&rdquo;
Ruda Patel looked bewildered until Amrutbai recounted the miraculous event—how the Lord had fed the entire congregation from two rotlas without diminishing the basket&rsquo;s contents in the slightest. Tears of reverence welled in Ruda Patel&rsquo;s eyes as he realized the elevated spiritual stature and boundless devotion of his wife.
Ruda Patel bowed humbly, &ldquo;O Lord! Forgive me; engrossed in farming chores, I was unaware of Your divine arrival!&rdquo;
Shreeji Maharaj comforted him warmly, &ldquo;Patel! You are our very own; worry not in the least. In fact, We intend to spend this entire night resting right here in your farm!&rdquo;
As dusk descended upon the farm, the saints and devotees filled the twilight air with soul-stirring arati, devotional kirtans, and divine discourses. When the time came to rest, Ruda Patel gathered soft sheaves of wheat straw (ghav na poola) and tender grass, covering them with white cotton sheets to fashion a plush, fragrant bed.
