Sadanand Swami from Patiya stood up and remarked: &ldquo;O Maharaj! What shall we say of habits? Atmanand Swami reprimands junior sadhus severely, yet when Anandanand Swami chewed &lsquo;chiya&rsquo;, he uttered not a word of admonition!&rdquo;
Shreeji Maharaj burst into gentle laughter and asked: &ldquo;Alaiya Khachar, what did you understand by the word &lsquo;chiya&rsquo;?&rdquo; Alaiya Khachar replied in earnest: &ldquo;O Maharaj! In our region of Zinzavadar, &lsquo;chiya&rsquo; refers to frogs!&rdquo; Maharaj clarified with delightful amusement: &ldquo;No, no, Alaiya Khachar! &lsquo;Chiya&rsquo; refers to dried tamarind seeds!&rdquo; Atmanand Swami humbly clarified that he had indeed questioned Anandanand Swami whether flatbreads were unavailable that he was gnawing on wooden seeds.
Maharaj then instituted a spiritual discipline regarding food intake, instructing sadhus to blend preparations when taking meals. Later, when female devotees arrived to listen to spiritual discourses, the ascetics instantly stood up and departed, strictly adhering to monastic boundaries. Commending their uncompromising moral purity, Shreehari affirmed: &ldquo;You have rightly imbibed scriptural injunctions!&rdquo;
Subsequently, scholarly ascetics posed a profound philosophical question from Vedanta: &ldquo;O Lord! Vedanta scriptures assert that &lsquo;Maya resides in one sector of Pure Consciousness (Chetan)&rsquo;. Yet Consciousness is all-pervading; which sector then houses Maya?&rdquo; Shreeji Maharaj offered an extraordinary illustration: &ldquo;Just as a magician transforms ropes into serpents and dust into grains of rice through innate magical expertise, that art pervades his entire being rather than resting in a specific hand or foot; similarly, Maya is situated within Pure Consciousness without spatial compartmentalization!&rdquo; Deeply enlightened, the congregation bowed in reverence.
