Goto Naviagation&#x1f447; English Jogidas Khuman and His Extraordinary Sense of Moral Restraint During the memorial ceremony (karaj) of Muktaraj Darbar Shri Jiva Bapu, the courageous Kathi Darbar Jogidas Khuman of Ambardi—who was living as an outlaw (baharvatiyo) at the time—came to Gadhpur. He arrived in the assembly to have the divine darshan of Shriji Maharaj. Shriji Maharaj then inquired of him before the entire gathering, &ldquo;Jogidas Khuman! When your eyes inadvertently fell upon another woman, you filled your eyes with chili powder as punishment. What was the reason behind this?&rdquo;
The spirited and noble Jogidas Khuman humbly replied, &ldquo;O Maharaj! My mother named me &lsquo;Jogidas Khuman&rsquo;—signifying that I am a humble servant of yogis and holy ascetics. How could I ever cast a wandering eye upon another woman? If I were to do so, it would bring shame to my mother&rsquo;s milk (her pure upbringing). Therefore, to punish my errant eyes, I filled them with chili powder.&rdquo;
Hearing this, Shriji Maharaj remarked to all the gathered sadhus, &ldquo;Behold, how profoundly he honors his name and reputation, thinking, &lsquo;I am known as a servant of yogis!&rsquo; If everyone upholds their integrity and moral restraint in this manner, then alone can Dharma be preserved; otherwise, Dharma cannot be sustained.&rdquo;
From Sadguru Shri Aksharanand Swami&rsquo;s Discourses (Vato 167)&hellip; 🙏
