Goto Naviagation&#x1f447; English Ratanji Chavda of Vataman and Shriji Maharaj In the village of Vataman located in the Bhal region, Ratanjibapu Chavda, an eternally liberated soul (purva na muktaraj), embraced the Satsang fellowship through the divine association of the holy sants. After receiving the divine darshan of Shri Hari, he lived in the world like a lotus upon water—completely unattached and unaffected by worldly entanglements.
Following the passing of Ratanjibapu’s wife, on the day of her post-funeral obsequies (karaj), Shri Hari arrived at his home in Vataman village accompanied by a large pilgrimage assembly (sangha). When Shri Hari set out to depart with the congregation, all the villagers walked along to bid Him farewell. After walking some distance, Shri Hari lovingly asked all the villagers to turn back. However, Ratanjibapu kept following behind and did not return even after reaching the boundary of the neighboring village. Seeing this, Shri Hari turned to him and said, &ldquo;Ratanji! Are you still following behind Us? What will you do by going back home now anyway? Come along with Us instead!&rdquo; Overjoyed, Ratanji replied with deep devotion, &ldquo;O Lord! I have no desire to return home anymore; now I wish only to stay in Your divine service forever.&rdquo;
Shri Hari welcomed him, and from that very moment, Ratanjibapu renounced his home, land, and possessions in an instant. For the rest of his life, he remained by Shri Hari’s side as His devoted bodyguard and personal attendant, following Him like an inseparable shadow. He never once looked back or returned to tend to his worldly household.
Ratanji understood Shri Hari&rsquo;s innermost wishes and served Him constantly with complete spiritual surrender (aatmabhav). In Dada Khachar&rsquo;s Darbargadh at Gadhada, he managed affairs with such heartfelt dedication and personal care as if he were the master of the house itself.
Once during the monsoon season, a torrential flash flood swept through the river Ghela. A young boy became stranded on a boulder right in the middle of the raging river current. The boy&rsquo;s father, weeping inconsolably, fell at Shri Hari&rsquo;s lotus feet and cried, &ldquo;O Lord! Please save my child from this furious flood!&rdquo; Shri Hari merely glanced toward Ratanji. Comprehending Shri Hari’s silent wish in an instant, without a moment’s hesitation or second thought, Ratanji leaped into the churning torrent of the Ghela. Battling the fierce currents, he reached the boy, lifted him safely, and brought him back to the riverbank. Pleased by Ratanji’s fearless and timely devotion, Shri Hari showered immense grace upon him.
During the fierce battle in the outskirts of Mandavdhar against the bandits Khabad and Matara, attendants like Bhaguji, Ratanji, and Miyaji demonstrated unmatched bravery and valor. Parshad Bhaguji and others were severely wounded, and Ratanji’s wrist was slashed. During those critical days, Shriji Maharaj visited the Darbargadh daily to personally attend to and check on the well-being of all the wounded parshads.
Thus, even at the risk of his own life, Ratanji never wavered in carrying out Shri Hari&rsquo;s divine commands.
Commemorating his exceptional dedication in Vachanamrut Ahmedabad 3, Shriji Maharaj remarked:
&ldquo;Are this Mulji Brahmachari and Ratanji worldly-wise? Yet they have a desperate yearning for salvation (kalyan no khap); therefore, they know how to act in ways that please God.&rdquo;
Furthermore, in Vachanamrut Gadhada Middle 41, Shri Hari affirmed:
&ldquo;To worship God devoid of ego and solely for the pleasure of God, only rare souls like this Miyaji and Ratanji can truly do so.&rdquo;
In his advanced years, Ratanji once visited Vadtal. One morning, after finishing his puja and devotions, Shukmuni’s disciple, Vamanprasad Swami, came by taking darshan of all. Observing the casual conduct of a young sant, Ratanji affectionately seated him close by and said, &ldquo;Please sit, let me share a word of loving guidance. Sadhus should not walk with their hands resting on each other&rsquo;s shoulders; such demeanor is a flaw for sadhus. Shriji Maharaj used to instruct us in this manner, which is why I mention it to you. Our sole endeavor must always remain focused on what pleases Maharaj.&rdquo;
Vamanprasad Swami deeply imbibed Ratanji’s virtuous counsel, expressed heartfelt gratitude, and performed prostrations (dandvat pranam) to him on the spot.
Parshadvarya Ratanji Chavda remained ceaselessly vigilant regarding his own spiritual discipline and conduct, striving every moment to earn the pleasure of Shri Hari. As an intimate parshad of Shriji Maharaj, he served selflessly throughout his life without the slightest desire for honor, fame, or recognition.
— From Mahapuja Muktagatha and Shri Haricharitra Chintamani&hellip;
