In the village of Hathsani, an arrogant and malicious ascetic (bawa) resided. Consistently intoxicated with cannabis and bhang, he created disturbances and practiced rampant hypocrisy. He harbored intense venom toward the sadhus of Bhagwan Swaminarayan. One day, when simple Swaminarayan sadhus entered Hathsani for spiritual preaching, this vile ascetic hurled foul abuses, mercilessly thrashed them, and drove them out of the village boundaries. Emboldened by his brutality, he marched to the central village square (chore) and bellowed arrogantly, &ldquo;As long as I draw breath, I will never permit these shaven-headed Swaminarayan sadhus to set foot in this village!&rdquo;
The local Kathi Darbar of Hathsani was a staunch devotee introduced to the faith by these very sadhus. Upon receiving word that an ascetic had assaulted the holy saints and chased them away, the Darbar was incandescent with fury. Mounting his swift mare, he galloped directly to the village square. Concealing his anger, he addressed the assembly in a deceptive tone: &ldquo;Who among you had the courage to beat those Swaminarayan sadhus? I wish to reward him handsomely!&rdquo;
Blinded by vain ego, the arrogant bawa stepped forward boastfully: &ldquo;Darbar! It was I who beat those ascetics at the village outskirts and drove them all the way to the river!&rdquo; To lure him into a definitive confession, the Darbar feigned disbelief: &ldquo;No, Babaji! A holy ascetic like you could never strike peaceful monks; it must surely be someone else.&rdquo; Puffing his chest, the bawa insisted, &ldquo;I swear by my word, Darbar! I personally thrashed those shaven-headed ascetics with my own hands!&rdquo;
Hearing this admission, the Darbar instantly pulled his mare&rsquo;s reins and struck the bawa&rsquo;s back with ferocious whips! Stunned and shrieking in agony, the bawa collapsed. The Darbar roared like a lion: &ldquo;You worthless rogue! Does this village belong to your father? Pack your belongings and flee this region immediately if you value your miserable life! Henceforth, if you ever utter an insult, harass, or touch any sadhu, consider your death written by my sword!&rdquo;
Terrified by the chieftain&rsquo;s blazing fury, the bawa fled the village in panic, never to return. The Darbar then rode swiftly to the riverbank, where the gentle sadhus had gathered. Falling at their sacred feet with folded hands, he pleaded for forgiveness and reverently escorted them back into the village. He hosted them at his manor for two days, served lavish freshly cooked meals, and organized spiritual discourses. When it was time for them to depart, he arranged a bullock cart and attendants to safely escort the sadhus on their journey.
In this manner, the valiant chieftain of Hathsani exemplified unyielding chivalry and deep devotion by fearlessly protecting the holy sadhus of God.
