श्रीहरिचरित्र चिंतामणि&hellip; 🙏 Goto Naviagation&#x1f447; English Parshad Diksha of Nilkanth Varni and Named Sarjudas in Loj Following His rigorous seven-year penance traversing the length and breadth of India, the young ascetic Nilkanth Varni arrived at the ashram of Sadguru Ramanand Swami in the quiet village of Loj. There, under the loving guidance of the senior disciple Sadguru Muktanand Swami, Varni resided among the assembly of fifty renunciants. Though He was the Supreme Almighty incarnate, He embraced the most menial ashram chores with profound humility. Every morning, holding a broom in His tender hands, He swept the ashram grounds spotless, weeded and watered the flowerbeds, collected cow dung from the barn, drew heavy buckets of water from the deep well, washed the saints&rsquo; robes, and begged for alms in the village. Yet beneath this relentless physical labor lay a single burning spiritual yearning—to meet His spiritual Guru, Sadguru Ramanand Swami. Repeatedly, He pleaded with Muktanand Swami: &ldquo;Revered Swami, pray devise some means whereby I may behold Gurudev Ramanand Swami!&rdquo;
Observing the young ascetic performing austerities clad merely in a rough bark-strip or ascetic loincloth (kopin), Sadguru Muktanand Swami lovingly advised Him one day: &ldquo;Dear Varniraj! We now reside in an ashram situated within a settled village, where householders and families regularly visit for spiritual instruction. In a social setting, living solely in an ascetic&rsquo;s loincloth is neither fitting nor customary. It would be most auspicious if You adorned a white dhoti, draped a clean upper cloth over Your shoulders, and embraced the proper attire of an initiated sadhu!&rdquo;
&ldquo;Adopt the traditional ascetic attire, and shave away Your long matted locks..!&rdquo;
Hearing the instruction of the senior saint, Nilkanth Varni folded His hands with supreme humility: &ldquo;O Swami! Your word is my absolute command; from this moment onward, I shall gladly wear robes.&rdquo; Delighted by Varni&rsquo;s total egolessness and instant obedience, Muktanand Swami gifted Him pristine white cotton cloths.
&ldquo;He honored the word of Muktamuni, living as an utterly egoless disciple!&rdquo;
Ever since His sacred thread ceremony (yagnopavit) in Chhapaiya at the age of eight, His head had remained unshaven; during His arduous seven-year wanderings across mountains, forests, and icy caves, His hair had grown into long, tangled matted locks (panch-kesh). Now, at the age of eighteen, at the Loj ashram, He had His matted ascetic locks ceremonially shaved.
Beholding the youthful yogi draped in spotless white dhoti and shoulder cloth, radiant with divine effulgence and purity, Sadguru Muktanand Swami&rsquo;s heart swelled with devotional ecstasy. He ceremoniously performed the sacred worship of Nilkanth Varni, anointing His forehead with fragrant sandalwood paste, garlanding Him with fresh blooms, and initiating Him into the order of novice attendants (parshad) with the holy name Sarjudas—the servant of God from the banks of the sacred Sarayu River!
&ldquo;He anointed His brow with fragrant chandan and conferred the holy name Sarjudas!
Named was He Sarjudas, and fifty rejoicing saints celebrated in bliss..!&rdquo;
All fifty ascetics of the ashram rejoiced in boundless spiritual joy. In eternal commemoration of this divine initiation ceremony of Lord Swaminarayan, the sacred victory chant is resoundingly proclaimed daily in Loj Mandir to this very day: &ldquo;Sarjudas Maharaj ni Jay!&rdquo;
