Goto Naviagation&#x1f447; English Bhago and Bhavan of Unjha: Fresh Cloves from Akshardham and Redemption from Ghosthood The town of Unjha in North Gujarat holds an esteemed place in early Swaminarayan history. It was here that Dinmani Sharma, an erudite scholar from Dantiya, was captivated by the divine brilliance of Shreeji Maharaj&rsquo;s radiant smile and teeth, subsequently being initiated as the stalwart Sadguru Nityanand Swami. Here too, Mahant Sevadasji of Kada near Kurukshetra received renunciation to become Sadguru Mahanubhavanand Swami, and the village lake was hallowed by Maharaj&rsquo;s water sports.
In this historic town lived two teenage brothers, fifteen-year-old Bhago and Bhavan. Daily, morning and evening, the young boys visited the temple. The moment the congregation commenced the chanting of the Swaminarayan Mahamantra, both boys would spontaneously lapse into deep spiritual trance (samadhi), remaining motionless for thirty to sixty minutes before awakening. Intrigued by this unusual occurrence, the village elders questioned them: &ldquo;Children, why do you fall fast asleep every time the dhun begins?&rdquo;
The boys replied with childlike innocence: &ldquo;We do not sleep! In samadhi, our souls are transported directly to the divine realm of Akshardham, where we revel in the bliss of beholding Shreeji Maharaj face-to-face.&rdquo;
Around that time, the first spiritual Acharya of the Northern Diocese, Ayodhyaprasadji Maharaj, arrived in Unjha. Village elders recounted the boys&rsquo; extraordinary trance experiences and requested: &ldquo;Revered Acharya Maharaj! Pray investigate this phenomenon yourself so the spiritual truth may be known.&rdquo;
During the evening assembly, Acharya Ayodhyaprasadji beckoned the boys near and inquired gently: &ldquo;If you truly transcend to Akshardham during samadhi, could you bring back some sanctified prasad from Shreeji Maharaj for us today?&rdquo; The boys asked, &ldquo;What prasad would you like us to bring, Maharajshri?&rdquo; An elder in the audience called out, &ldquo;Bring fresh, tender green cloves (which grow nowhere in these arid regions)!&rdquo; The boys agreed: &ldquo;Certainly! We will convey your prayer to Maharaj.&rdquo;
As the divine chanting reverberated through the hall, both brothers plunged into profound samadhi. In Akshardham, they prostrated before Bhagwan Swaminarayan and petitioned: &ldquo;O Supreme Lord! Your divine son, Acharya Ayodhyaprasadji Maharaj, has arrived at our temple in Unjha and has requested fresh green cloves as prasad from You!&rdquo;
Smiling benevolently, Shreeji Maharaj filled both their cupped palms with fresh, aromatic green cloves plucked directly in the spiritual realm. Maharaj also instructed: &ldquo;Convey to Ayodhyaprasadji that he suffers from a chronic phlegmatic constitution (shardi). Have him temper a pot of Gujarati kadhi with these celestial cloves, offer it to Thakorji, and partake of it. His chronic cold and congestion will be cured permanently from this day forth!&rdquo;
When the chanting ended, the boys awakened from their trance. Opening their hands, they placed overflowing fistfuls of fragrant, tender green cloves at the holy feet of Acharya Ayodhyaprasadji, delivering Maharaj&rsquo;s exact medicinal and dietary counsel.
The Acharya and the entire congregation were spellbound by this undeniable divine miracle. Overjoyed, Acharya Ayodhyaprasadji distributed the celestial cloves among the devotees and then asked the boys: &ldquo;How did you two attain such effortless samadhi at this young age? Did you perform arduous penances in a past life? Tomorrow, pray ask Shreeji Maharaj in Akshardham to reveal your past spiritual background.&rdquo;
The following evening, as the dhun commenced, the boys once again entered samadhi, reached Akshardham, and placed the question before Shreeji Maharaj. Maharaj placed His compassionate hand upon their heads and unveiled their past. When the boys returned from samadhi, they broke down into inconsolable weeping. Acharya Ayodhyaprasadji gently consoled them and inquired about the cause of their tears.
Wiping their eyes, the brothers recounted: &ldquo;Acharya Maharaj! In our previous birth, we were greedy moneylending merchants (Vaniyas). Driven by avarice, we fraudulently usurped a poor, helpless farmer&rsquo;s mango orchard through predatory loans. The brokenhearted farmer&rsquo;s agonizing curses fell upon our household. Soon, a bitter dispute erupted between us two brothers over the possession of that very land. Quarrelling viciously over a prized perennial (baramasi) mango tree, we struck and murdered each other in cold blood! As retribution for that horrific sin, we were condemned to become agonizing wandering ghosts (preta), trapped inside that very mango tree for decades.&rdquo;
They continued through tears of gratitude: &ldquo;One sweltering afternoon, Sadguru Muktanand Swami and his saintly retinue happened to travel down that country road and sat beneath that very mango tree to rest for a brief hour. By the mere physical proximity of that enlightened saint, the touch of the holy dust from his feet, and overhearing their divine spiritual discourses, our mountain of demonic sins was instantly dissolved! Through the unearned grace of that Sadguru, we were liberated from ghosthood and granted human birth into a pious satsangi home, bestowed with effortless trance into Akshardham!&rdquo;
Hearing how a brief, casual contact with a pure saint had redeemed murderous ghosts into blissful residents of Akshardham, the entire assembly was submerged in reverence for the glory of Satsang.
