Goto Naviagation&#x1f447; English The Poor Brahmin&rsquo;s Coarse Bread and Luni Greens in Vadtal During His holy stay in Vadtal, Bhagwan Swaminarayan resided in a consecrated room (oredi) belonging to a carpenter disciple. Every day, vibrant spiritual assemblies gathered beneath the sprawling shade of the sacred ambla trees. Devotional pilgrim groups (sanghs) flooded in from distant towns, bathing in the joyous monsoon of Satsang. Twice daily, elevating spiritual discourses were delivered. At noon, Mulji Brahmachari would prepare a rich devotional meal (thal) in the oredi, which Maharaj partook of with serene grace. Devotees and visiting ascetics camped comfortably under the mango groves, and Maharaj would occasionally grace the community kitchens of visiting pilgrims to relish their offerings.
During this vibrant gathering, an impoverished, destitute Brahmin arrived in Vadtal seeking the Lord&rsquo;s divine association. Throughout the day, he immersed his heart in the discourses and soaked in the darshan of Shreeji Maharaj. At noon, he would quietly wander through the town streets seeking alms (bhikshavritti), sustaining himself solely on whatever flour was donated by generous householders.
One afternoon, having collected a small handful of mixed, coarse five-grain flour (panchkani), he sat by a rustic hearth in the outskirts of the orchard. Gathering wild pursuit greens (luni ni bhaji) from the nearby fields, he boiled them into a simple broth and patted the coarse dough into a thick flatbread (rotlo). As he tended the earthen griddle, a tender, sorrowful lament welled up from the depths of his soul: &ldquo;O merciful Lord! Wealthy devotees present You with sumptuous feasts of clarified butter and exquisite sweets. When will a wretched, penniless pauper like me ever have the fortune to serve You a meal? I possess nothing worthy in this entire world to lay at Your holy feet!&rdquo; Weeping silently, he felt crushed by his apparent insignificance.
At that very moment in the private chamber, Mulji Brahmachari had laid out a lavish, freshly cooked royal banquet for Maharaj. Sending word to the assembly, he pleaded: &ldquo;O Maharaj! The noon hour has arrived; the sanctified thal is ready. Pray grace the dining seat.&rdquo;
Maharaj rose from the congregation and stepped into the chamber. But as Brahmachari placed the golden platters forward, the omniscient Lord said: &ldquo;Brahmachari! Bring My water pot; today We must proceed far beyond the orchards to answer nature&rsquo;s call. We shall eat only after We return.&rdquo;
Mulji Brahmachari protested anxiously: &ldquo;Maharaj! Why walk out into the scorching sun? Relieve Yourself nearby and bathe here!&rdquo;
Maharaj replied firmly: &ldquo;No, today an unavoidable duty calls Us far into the outskirts. If We are delayed, go ahead and take your meal without waiting for Us!&rdquo; Uttering these words, Maharaj hurried off alone toward the edge of the groves.
Just as the humble Brahmin lifted the steaming, coarse five-grain rotlo from the earthen griddle, Shreeji Maharaj suddenly stood before him, His lotus face beaming with radiance! Maharaj addressed him warmly: &ldquo;Bhudev! We are overwhelmed by fierce hunger; bring whatever food you have prepared without delay!&rdquo;
The Brahmin was struck dumb with ecstatic wonder. Trembling with awe, he swiftly spread his faded, tattered outer cloth upon the ground and invited Maharaj to be seated. With folded hands and a quavering voice, he confessed: &ldquo;O Sovereign of the Cosmos! I am but a destitute mendicant living on alms. I have only this coarse, unsalted five-grain rotlo and boiled wild luni greens. How could the Lord of the Universe partake of such lowly peasant fare?&rdquo;
Maharaj smiled affectionately: &ldquo;Serve it immediately, Bhudev! For today, We have a hunger that only your offering can satisfy!&rdquo;
With overflowing tears of unadulterated love, the Brahmin offered the rustic bread and wild greens. Breaking off hearty morsels, Shreeji Maharaj relished the humble meal with unmistakable relish, exclaiming between bites: &ldquo;Ah, Bhudev! Truly, today your offering carries the sublime, divine sweetness of the wild greens that Vidurji served Me in the Dwapar Yuga!&rdquo;
Leaving behind rich culinary splendors, the Supreme Lord once again validated that He hungers not for royal banquets, but for the untainted love and devotion of a pure heart.
