A short distance away, beneath another banyan tree, a British colonial military officer sat resting with four Indian armed sepoys. Watching from afar, the soldiers were thoroughly mystified to see a leader of such regal bearing and effulgent aura personally stoking the fire, cooking, and humbly waiting on His followers like an ordinary servant!
Driven by curiosity, the sepoys strolled over toward the camp. Spotting them approach, Sura Khachar hailed them in his signature Kathiyawadi drawl: &ldquo;Brothers! Come closer over here; let us introduce you!&rdquo;
The sepoys pointed towards the radiant figure of Shreeji Maharaj and asked: &ldquo;Sir, who is this distinguished personage?&rdquo;
With a deadpan expression, Sura Khachar replied: &ldquo;He is the Commander-in-Chief (Fojdar) of our regiment!&rdquo;
The sepoys gazed upon Maharaj&rsquo;s tranquil, magnetic countenance with profound reverence, marveling: &ldquo;Your Commander is extraordinarily gracious! He personally cooks the khichdi with His own hands and serves it to His men with love! Our British commander does nothing but yell at us, throw his weight around, and boss us harshly. Tell us, Bapu, would your Commander be willing to enlist us into His regiment?&rdquo;
Sura Khachar replied enthusiastically: &ldquo;Of course! Our Commander would gladly enlist you all into His ranks! But you must understand our salary structure: as for board, you receive delicious meals and refreshing water daily; but as for monthly cash pay—exactly zero paise! In this regiment, we serve purely for spiritual detachment!&rdquo;
The moment they heard &ldquo;zero pay,&rdquo; the materialistically minded sepoys turned on their heels and bolted in terror!
Watching the fleeing sepoys and Sura Khachar&rsquo;s playful wit, Shreeji Maharaj and the entire congregation erupted into unrestrained laughter. Beneath the shade of the ancient banyan in Amod, this delightful episode of divine humor was immortalized in the annals of Satsang history.
