Goto Naviagation&#x1f447; English Ratanba&rsquo;s Selfless Seva of Sweeping the Road and Kantola Thal in Gadhpur In the holy town of Gadhada (Gadhpur), an elderly widow named Ratanba led a humble existence. Destitute and bereft of any surviving family, she sustained herself by performing odd manual labor. Despite her poverty, her heart was filled with boundless reverence and divine love for Bhagwan Swaminarayan and His ascetic fellowship. She often reflected with poignant humility: &ldquo;I possess no material wealth to offer in the lavish festivities at the Darbargadh, but surely I can offer the sweat and strength of my mortal body in selfless devotion!&rdquo;
Consequently, she established a remarkable daily discipline. Long before dawn, during the sacred early hours of Brahma-muhurta, she arose with a simple hand broom. Starting from the grand wooden gates of Dada Khachar’s Darbargadh, she diligently swept the entire rocky dirt trail leading all the way to Lakshmiwadi and the bathing ghats of River Ghela. Her humble intent was twofold: to protect the tender, barefoot ascetics from sharp stones and thorns as they headed to the river for their ritual morning bath, and to silently drink in the purifying sight (darshan) of the holy paramhansas.
One morning, having finished His early morning prayers, Shriji Maharaj mounted His beloved mare Manki and set out towards Lakshmiwadi. Spotting Ratanba bending low with her broom along the freshly cleaned road, Maharaj halted beside her and called out with touching warmth, &ldquo;O mother! By sweeping this path every dawn, you have truly earned the purest divine pleasure of all the holy saints and devotees!&rdquo;
Tears pooled in Ratanba’s eyes as she folded her trembling hands and replied, &ldquo;O Maharaj! Splendid festivals take place daily in the Darbargadh where wealthy devotees offer heaps of silver and gold coins. But this unfortunate soul has nothing of monetary value to give. My heart yearned that by sweeping the path for the holy sadhus, the saints and You would be pleased, thereby redeeming this fragile human birth.&rdquo;
Smiling benevolently, Maharaj observed a bulge tied in her faded garment and asked, &ldquo;Mother, what have you tied to the edge of your sari pallu?&rdquo;
Blushing with shyness, Ratanba replied, &ldquo;Maharaj, I plucked a handful of wild spiny gourds (kantola) from the thorny bushes alongside the path to deliver to Jivuba for the temple kitchen.&rdquo;
Shriji Maharaj immediately stretched out the hem of His upper garment (khes) and insisted, &ldquo;Hand them to Us! Know that We have lovingly accepted your seva right here!&rdquo;
Returning to the Darbargadh later that morning, Maharaj visited the royal kitchen where Jivuba and Laduba were preparing the noon offering. Maharaj asked, &ldquo;What delicacy are you cooking for Our midday meal (thal) today?&rdquo; Jivuba replied humbly, &ldquo;Maharaj, whatever You desire, we shall prepare.&rdquo;
The affectionate Lord presented the wild vegetables and said, &ldquo;Take these fresh kantola gourds; prepare a sabji out of them so that Ratanba&rsquo;s earnest prayer of offering food to God is fulfilled!&rdquo; Maharaj then specially instructed Jivuba and Laduba: &ldquo;Engage Ratanba in whatever gentle service she can manage in the Darbargadh and provide her daily meals with love. Exactly six months from today, We shall personally come and welcome her to Akshardham!&rdquo;
Through her unpretentious service, the impoverished widow exemplified how pure, unalloyed devotion outweighs all the worldly riches in the eyes of God.
