Goto Naviagation&#x1f447; English The Test of Vaji Ba in Vijapur: Shriji Maharaj&rsquo;s Bava Disguise and Divine Feet In the town of Vijapur, there lived a devoted couple, Vaji Ba and her husband Pragjibhai Sathwara, who were supreme devotees of Bhagwan Swaminarayan. Initially, wandering ascetics and bawas used to lodge at their home. However, when Sadguru Bhairamdas Swami visited Vijapur and enlightened them with the supreme glory of Shriji Maharaj, their household transformed completely. The very home that once hosted hemp-smoking ascetics became a sanctuary of pure devotion, Satsang assemblies, and divine spiritual wisdom.
On one occasion, while travelling towards Viramgam with a large congregation of devotees, Shriji Maharaj halted outside Vijapur. Turning to the assembly, Maharaj instructed, &ldquo;All of you make camp and spend the night here outside the village. Mukund Brahmachari and I will return tomorrow morning.&rdquo;
Shriji Maharaj then assumed the guise of an austere ascetic (bava). He donned dark, matted locks, an ochre patchwork robe (kantha), and carried a brass water-pot (kamandalu) and iron tongs (chimto). He instructed Mukund Brahmachari, &ldquo;Follow quietly behind Me and do not utter a single syllable until morning!&rdquo;
Seeking out Vaji Ba’s dwelling, Maharaj entered the courtyard under the veil of dusk. Her husband, Pragjibhai, was away keeping night vigil at their agricultural field. As the disguised bava entered the courtyard, Vaji Ba sternly stopped Him at the entrance: &ldquo;We no longer entertain wandering bawas at our residence at this hour! Go to the village rest-house (choro)!&rdquo;
Undeterred, Maharaj placed His bag on the veranda and demanded alms of millet flatbread (rotla) and milk. Vaji Ba retorted sharply that no food remained and the kitchen fire was extinguished for the night. Maharaj teased, &ldquo;Mother! Why do you speak untruth? In your kitchen container, a fresh stack of hot bajra rotlas is kept, and a bowl full of rich milk awaits! Bring them out and serve Us!&rdquo;
Surprised that the stranger knew precisely what was hidden in her kitchen, Vaji Ba suspected he was an occultist. Reluctantly, she served four rotlas and two bowls of milk, which Maharaj and the Brahmachari ate.
Shortly thereafter, Maharaj spoke again: &ldquo;Mother, sleep eludes Us on this bare earthen veranda; fetch a cot for Us.&rdquo; Irritated, Vaji Ba scolded, &ldquo;If you craved such luxuries, why renounce family life? Look at our Swaminarayan sadhus—they ask for nothing and eat only once a day!&rdquo;
Maharaj casually added, &ldquo;In the third inner room, upon the loft, two warm quilts are placed. Fetch those as well!&rdquo; Astonished by his uncanny inner vision, she brought out the cot and bedding. Maharaj reposed upon the cot while Mukund Brahmachari slept on the veranda floor.
Late in the night, curiosity seized Vaji Ba. Wondering about the enigmatic young ascetic who seemed omniscient, she peered through the bedroom window in the dim lantern light. What she witnessed took her breath away: the bava&rsquo;s two legs had miraculously elongated to a length of twenty cubits (nearly thirty feet), stretching across the courtyard, over the well, and resting against the trunk of the peepal tree! Awestruck yet bewildered, she retreated to bed, unable to comprehend the divine miracle. By dawn, the cot was empty; the two mendicants had quietly departed.
A year later, a large group of pilgrims from Vijapur visited Gadhada, and Vaji Ba was among them. During the royal court in the Darbargadh, Shriji Maharaj reclined gracefully on His divan and extended His divine lotus feet toward Vaji Ba. With a radiant smile, Maharaj asked, &ldquo;Ba, do you recognize these lotus feet? These are the very same feet that extended all the way to the peepal tree in your courtyard that night!&rdquo;
The realization struck Vaji Ba like a thunderbolt. Overwhelmed with profound remorse and devotion, she wept profusely and pleaded, &ldquo;O Maharaj! How ignorant and blind I was not to recognize You! The Supreme Lord Himself visited my doorstep, asked for food three times, and I subjected You to harsh reprimands!&rdquo;
With boundless compassion, Shriji Maharaj consoled her: &ldquo;Vaji Ba! Your unflinching loyalty and unbending devotion to Satsang surpass even the steadfastness of cosmic deities like Brahma and Shiva. It was to personally witness and celebrate your resolute conviction that We orchestrated this divine leela!&rdquo;
