Goto Naviagation&#x1f447; English Deha Charan&rsquo;s Quest for a Guru and Becoming Krishnasevadas Swami From the arid lands of Marwar (Rajasthan), an earnest seeker of spiritual truth named Deha Charan embarked on a quest to find a genuine God-realized saint (Siddha Purush) capable of granting him liberation and direct communion with the Supreme Lord. Having heard legends that the mystical peaks and secluded caverns of Mount Girnar in Junagadh had sheltered holy ascetics, yogis, and masters since time immemorial, Deha Charan journeyed across western India to the foot of Girnar. He climbed its thousand steps and explored its remote caves, hoping to meet an enlightened soul. Instead, he encountered only worldly, arrogant, and hypocritical mendicants who craved praise and money. Brokenhearted, he lamented with an ancient folk verse:
&ldquo;Janku dekhi chhati thare, vanka padya dukaal&rdquo; (Alas, there is a severe famine of those holy souls whose mere sight brings eternal peace to the burning heart!)
Despairing of finding a true master, Deha Charan turned his steps back toward Marwar. Traversing the dusty countryside near Dhoraji in Saurashtra during a scorching afternoon, he paused at a roadside agricultural well where a water-wheel was operating. He washed his dusty face and sat under the soothing canopy of a dense tree to rest. Nearby, he noticed an imposing figure seated in deep transcendental meditation—a tall, stately youth dressed in pristine white garments, sporting an effulgent saffron Chandlo enclosed within a yellow sandalwood Tilak upon his broad forehead. Intrigued by the extraordinary aura of peace radiating from him, Deha Charan waited patiently.
When the meditative trance ended, Deha Charan greeted him with folded hands: &ldquo;Greetings, noble sir! I am Deha, a Charan by caste from Marwar. I set out to search for a living saint on Mount Girnar who could unite me with God, but found none. Disheartened, I am returning home.&rdquo;
The radiant youth smiled warmly and introduced himself: &ldquo;I am Abhesinh, the ruler (Darbar) of Lodhika. Let me tell you: the divine masters who redeemed a sinful and worldly soul like mine and introduced me to manifest God are Sadguru Gopalanand Swami and Sadguru Gunatitanand Swami! They are presently residing at the Swaminarayan Mandir in Junagadh itself! Go to them immediately; they will surely grant you salvation!&rdquo;
A surge of fresh life coursed through Deha Charan. Obtaining a written note of introduction from Darbar Abhesinh, he immediately changed direction and hurried back toward Junagadh with renewed hope.
The instant he set foot inside the sacred precinct of the Junagadh Swaminarayan temple, a sublime wave of coolness and profound serenity washed over his weary soul. Seated by a wooden pillar in the assembly hall sat Sadguru Gunatitanand Swami. Overwhelmed by an innate certainty that his search was over, Deha Charan fell prostrate at Swami’s lotus feet. The omniscient master looked upon him with infinite motherly affection and welcomed him: &ldquo;Come, Deha Charan! You have wandered enough through this mundane world; now reside right here!&rdquo;
Hearing his name uttered without introduction, Deha Charan surrendered completely. He never returned to worldly life. Subsequently, Sadguru Gunatitanand Swami initiated him into the holy paramhansa order, naming him Sadguru Krishnasevadas Swami. He served as Gunatitanand Swami’s foremost personal attendant for the rest of his life, epitomizing the glory of selfless surrender and guru-bhakti.
