Goto Naviagation&#x1f447; English Supreme Sacrifice of a Poor Widow in Balagam: Dedicating Both Sons to Sainthood While Bhagwan Swaminarayan was gracing Panchala with His divine presence, devotees from Balagam—a quiet rural hamlet nearby—earnestly pleaded with Him to sanctify their village. In Balagam, an impoverished elderly widow had quietly nurtured the seeds of faith, introducing several local women to the pure devotional tenets of Satsang. Responding to their sincere devotion, Shriji Maharaj arrived in Balagam, establishing His residence at the communal village rest-house (choro) and personally undertaking holy home visits (padhramani) to every devotee&rsquo;s dwelling.
This elderly widow lived in abject poverty. Having lost her husband years earlier, she shouldered the sole responsibility of raising her two young sons with negligible means. Yet, when she received the joyful news that the Supreme Lord had stepped into their modest hamlet, poverty ceased to matter; she was transported into ecstatic jubilation.
Presently, a fellow devotee hurried to her doorstep: &ldquo;Mother! Shriji Maharaj Himself is walking down the lane to bless your humble hut!&rdquo; The widow&rsquo;s heart leaped with joy. As Maharaj entered her humble abode, she performed His arti with pure, trembling love and showered Him with sanctified grains of rice. In her heart, a solemn resolution was born: &ldquo;The sovereign of all realms has crossed my threshold today; I must offer Him something truly precious!&rdquo;
Yet, casting her eyes around the earthen walls, she beheld only absolute penury: a few earthen cooking pots, a creaky rope-cot, and worn, threadbare rags. There was not a grain of gold or a single coin to present.
Seating Maharaj gently upon the cot, she bowed low and said with spontaneous devotion: &ldquo;Maharaj! Ask of me whatever You wish today; whatever You demand, I shall gladly surrender to You!&rdquo;
The Lord of countless cosmoses, who effortlessly sustains every living being from the tiniest ant to the mightiest elephant, gazed tenderly upon the impoverished woman and said: &ldquo;O Mother! Give Us whatever is most precious and beloved to your heart!&rdquo;
The widow searched her home, but found nothing of material worth. Stepping into the courtyard, she saw her solitary cow tethered by a wooden peg. She initially thought of offering the cow so its milk could nourish the travelling sadhus. But her heart yearned deeper: &ldquo;Can I not find something infinitely more dear to lay at the lotus feet of God, thereby making this mortal existence truly sublime?&rdquo;
At that precise moment, her two young sons ran in from playing outside, their faces flushed with childhood innocence. The instant her gaze fell upon her children, a surge of supreme surrender overtook her maternal heart. Taking both boys by the hand, she led them directly before Shriji Maharaj, laid their hands into His divine palms, and declared with unshakeable resolve: &ldquo;O Maharaj! In this entire universe, nothing is dearer to this mother&rsquo;s heart than these two children. Take them, my Lord; accept them both in Your holy service!&rdquo;
Beholding the supreme, heartbreaking sacrifice of this destitute widow, tears of profound divine appreciation filled Shriji Maharaj’s eyes. Overwhelmed by her unreserved surrender, Maharaj said with deep emotion: &ldquo;Mother! Blessed are you, truly blessed! Your supreme surrender is peerless in all the worlds!&rdquo;
Accepting her offering, Shriji Maharaj initiated both young boys into the monastic paramhansa order, naming them Sadhu Daharanand and Sadhu Prasadanand. Without a moment’s hesitation, a destitute mother had consecrated the only solace and support of her old age to the service of God, immortalizing her faith in the annals of devotion.
