Goto Naviagation&#x1f447; English Playful Milk Stream by Jodha Bhagat in Gadhpur and the Tale of the Titodi Bird One pleasant evening in Gadhada, Shriji Maharaj was strolling from His sacred residence, the Akshar Ordi, towards the main courtyard of Jiva Bapu’s Darbargadh. Along the path, His guileless and intimate devotee, Jodha Bhagat, was sitting on a low wooden stool, engrossed in milking his cow into a brass pail.
Fond of orchestrating spontaneous, endearing pastimes with His inner circle of devotees, Shriji Maharaj approached quietly from behind. In a playful impulse, Maharaj tapped Jodha Bhagat on the back with the pointed toe of His royal embroidered slipper (mojdi). Without missing a beat or even looking back, Jodha Bhagat skillfully angled the cow’s udder and sent a powerful, direct jet of warm milk straight onto Shriji Maharaj!
The white stream splattered directly across Maharaj’s resplendent zari-brocaded tunic (jama) and fine silk trousers (surval)! Startled, Maharaj leaped back, energetically dusting His drenched robes. Feigning indignation, He exclaimed: &ldquo;Jodha! You haven’t reformed in the slightest! You are still as incorrigibly mischievous and ill-mannered as ever!&rdquo;
Ceasing his milking, Jodha Bhagat looked up with complete rustic candidness and cheekily fired back: &ldquo;O Maharaj! Compared to Your antics, my manners are far superior! Just to honor the stubborn grievance of a tiny lapwing bird (titodi), You submerged the entire cosmos in a cosmic deluge, yet You dare complain that I haven’t reformed?!&rdquo;
Amused by his witty retort, Maharaj asked with raised eyebrows: &ldquo;Hey Jodha, and who on earth told you all these deep scriptural allegories?&rdquo;
Jodha Bhagat proudly answered with absolute conviction: &ldquo;My venerable Guru, Sadguru Muktanand Swami! He was explaining in the holy assembly just how immensely great and all-powerful our Maharaj is!&rdquo;
Hearing this delightfully unfiltered, innocent display of devotional intimacy, Shriji Maharaj was thoroughly charmed and burst into joyous, affectionate laughter. This playful banter echoes the deep devotional sentiments captured in the classic verse:
&ldquo;Girishikhar par titodi bethi gaati gordhandhari&hellip;! Ek same ene trusha laagi tame payu vitthalvar vaari&hellip;! Bana ni pat rakho raj, eva guna amara lakho raj&hellip;&rdquo; (“Upon the mountain summit perched the little lapwing, singing praises of the lifter of Mount Govardhan! When thirst afflicted her, the Lord Himself quenched her with sweet water! O King of Kings, uphold the honor of Your title as the protector of the fallen, for our faults and foibles are numbered in the millions!”)
