Goto Naviagation&#x1f447; English Daha Khachar of Botad: Realizing Divine Glory via Adbhutanand Swami&rsquo;s Sacred Cloth During his continuous spiritual travels across Saurashtra, Sadguru Adbhutanand Swami, one of Bhagwan Swaminarayan’s prominent Paramhansas, arrived at the Darbargadh (royal estate) of Darbar Daha Khachar in Botad. Although Daha Khachar was a recognized devotee who acknowledged Shriji Maharaj as divine and frequently hosted Maharaj and the traveling ascetics at his estate, his devotional understanding was relatively superficial; the true cosmic and transcendental greatness (mahima) of the manifest Lord had not yet fully penetrated his consciousness.
Early the following morning, Sadguru Adbhutanand Swami completed his ritual ablutions and sat down to perform his daily morning worship (puja). Daha Khachar came and sat reverently beside Swami to observe. During the puja, Swami reverently removed a small piece of cloth from his pouch—a fragment of a sanctified garment worn by Shriji Maharaj (prasadi vastra). With intense devotion, Swami pressed the sacred cloth to his eyes and forehead, gently placing it in the sanctum of his puja. As he meditated upon the divine murti of Maharaj, profound spiritual emotion surged through Swami&rsquo;s heart, and a continuous stream of tears flowed down his cheeks. He became completely immersed in divine bliss. Daha Khachar watched this spectacle with wide-eyed astonishment.
When Swami completed his prayers, Daha Khachar could no longer contain his perplexity. He inquired: &ldquo;O Swami! You took that small rag of cloth, pressed it with such veneration to your eyes, and became totally overwhelmed with weeping emotion! I cannot comprehend this; pray explain it clearly so that this ignorant soul may understand!&rdquo;
Sadguru Adbhutanand Swami looked up and said with profound affection: &ldquo;Darbar! You are exceptionally fortunate. Whenever a longing arises in your heart, you can simply mount your horse, ride down to Gadhada, behold Shriji Maharaj face-to-face, and celebrate glorious festivals in His holy presence. But we ascetics are bound by His divine commands to wander and preach; often a year or two elapses before we are permitted to return for His personal darshan! Therefore, whenever I place this sanctified piece of His robe in my daily puja, I contemplate it with the living conviction that I am beholding the direct, manifest presence of Supreme Lord Purushottam Himself!&rdquo;
Swami&rsquo;s heartfelt words struck Daha Khachar&rsquo;s heart like an awakening flash of divine light. A profound realization dawned upon him: &ldquo;If a mere fragment of Maharaj&rsquo;s garment carries such unfathomable sanctity and cosmic power, then the noble horses in my stables which Maharaj rode, the royal mansions and verandas where He sat, the bullocks He caressed, and the mattresses upon which He rested are all saturated with the supreme divinity of God!&rdquo; The flame of spiritual understanding was ignited within him.
Unable to remain in Botad even for another moment, Daha Khachar immediately mounted his swiftest steed and galloped at breakneck speed towards Gadhpur. Rushing into the Darbargadh courtyard, he cast aside all royal pride and repeatedly performed fervent, breathless prostrations (dandvats) at Shriji Maharaj’s lotus feet.
The omniscient Lord smiled knowingly and quipped: &ldquo;Darbar! It appears you crossed paths with one of Our genuine sadhus today; otherwise, such an unyielding stone would never have melted like this!&rdquo;
With folded hands and tears streaming down his face, Daha Khachar confessed: &ldquo;O Maharaj! Without Your divine sadhus, who could ever unveil Your true transcendent glory to worldly mortals like us? Today, Sadguru Adbhutanand Swami made me comprehend Your real greatness; otherwise, my entire human existence would have been utterly wasted!&rdquo;
Shriji Maharaj beamed with divine pleasure and proclaimed: &ldquo;Today, by renouncing the world as a monk for the third time across his spiritual journey, he has truly lived up to his holy name, &lsquo;Adbhutanand&rsquo; (Wondrous Bliss)!&rdquo;
