Goto Naviagation&#x1f447; English Assuming the Form of a Shepherd from Mandavdhar, Shreeji Maharaj Procures Milk for a Feast and Bestows Darshan as Lord Krishna Once in Gadhpur, Sadguru Brahmanand Swami and Sura Khachar playfully asked Shreeji Maharaj, &ldquo;O Maharaj! You continually have various devotees sponsor feasts for the sadhus, but when will You personally host a grand feast for us from Your own hands?&rdquo;
Maharaj smiled and replied, &ldquo;Dear brothers, every feast that is arranged is indeed arranged solely through My divine inspiration!&rdquo;
However, Brahmanand Swami and Sura Khachar affectionately insisted: &ldquo;Maharaj, please sponsor a feast of delicious dudhpak (rich milk pudding) and puris for all of us!&rdquo;
Maharaj smiled warmly and made a promise: &ldquo;Very well! I wish to gather milk from a single household to feed the holy saints, but after one month; not right away.&rdquo;
Twenty-five days passed without Maharaj mentioning the subject. Brahmanand Swami reminded Him: &ldquo;Maharaj, the month is almost over; on which day will You arrange the feast?&rdquo;
When just a day remained, an affluent Rabari (cowherd) belonging to the neighboring village of Mandavdhar was preparing to lead his herds grazing toward the northern Vadhiyar pastures. Before departing, he strictly warned his family members: &ldquo;Be vigilant! If the followers or sadhus of Swaminarayan come asking for milk, do not give them even a single drop!&rdquo;
The omniscient Shreeji Maharaj thought to Himself: &lsquo;I shall obtain the milk for the feast precisely from his home.&rsquo; Immediately, through His illimitable divine powers, Shreeji Maharaj assumed the exact form and voice of that very cowherd and walked into the house in Mandavdhar!
Seeing her husband return so quickly, his wife asked in astonishment: &ldquo;Why have you returned so soon? You had said you would be away for fifteen days!&rdquo;
Maharaj (in the cowherd&rsquo;s guise) feigned anxiety and explained: &ldquo;As I was traveling, an enormous serpent came charging directly toward me! In terror, I climbed up a large neem tree. The serpent coiled around the base of the tree and spread its venomous hood right facing me! If I dared descend, it would bite me fatally. In that dire crisis, I took a vow: &lsquo;If this serpent slithers away unharmed, I will offer a feast of dudhpak to Bhagwan Swaminarayan!&rsquo; The moment I made this pledge, the serpent vanished! Therefore, I have rushed back to instruct you: whatever seven to eight maunds (mounds) of milk our cows yield early tomorrow morning, milk them promptly and deliver it all to the Darbargadh in Gadhada!&rdquo;
The obedient wife agreed: &ldquo;Very well, rest assured, I shall send it early in the morning.&rdquo;
Overhearing their conversation, the cowherd’s younger brother entered and asked: &ldquo;Sister-in-law, what are you discussing?&rdquo; She narrated: &ldquo;Your brother encountered a deadly crisis and barely escaped with his life. He made a solemn vow to deliver all our morning milk to Bhagwan Swaminarayan.&rdquo; The younger brother readily offered: &ldquo;Of course! In that case, I will gladly add all the milk from my cows as well!&rdquo;
Early the next morning, both herds were milked, and large brass pots brimming with fresh milk were brought into the royal court in Gadhpur. Seeing the pots arrive, Brahmanand Swami and Sura Khachar smiled knowing: &ldquo;Maharaj has entered someone&rsquo;s heart through His divine power to summon this milk!&rdquo;
Maharaj instructed: &ldquo;Empty the pots into the cooking cauldrons!&rdquo; A rich, sweet dudhpak was prepared and served with warm wheat rotlis to all the delighted sadhus, fulfilling Maharaj&rsquo;s pledge.
A few days later, the real cowherd returned from Vadhiyar with his cattle. The very first question he asked was: &ldquo;I hope you didn&rsquo;t give any milk to Swaminarayan, did you?&rdquo;
The wife retorted in bewilderment: &ldquo;Why, you yourself came rushing back and commanded us to deliver all the milk because of the snake vow!&rdquo;
The cowherd protested: &ldquo;I expressly forbade you before leaving; when did I ever return?&rdquo;
When his wife and brother recounted the entire conversation, the bewildered and furious cowherd declared: &ldquo;Come with me, we are going to Gadhada right now to confront Swaminarayan about why He deceitfully took our milk!&rdquo;
Upon arriving at the Darbargadh in Gadhada, they found Shreeji Maharaj seated majestically in the royal spiritual assembly. The cowherd demanded: &ldquo;Swaminarayan! Why did You take our milk?&rdquo;
Maharaj replied with serene sweetness: &ldquo;Brother! When your own family members came willingly to offer the milk, We accepted it. When did We ever come to your doorstep to demand it?&rdquo;
As He spoke, Shreeji Maharaj cast a merciful glance upon the bewildered cowherds and manifested before their eyes the glorious, transcendent, four-armed vision of Lord Shri Krishna! Overwhelmed by the divine spectacle, all their anger, skepticism, and worldly vanity melted away into pure devotional ecstasy. They bowed down prostrate at Shreeji Maharaj’s lotus feet, received initiation (vartman) into the Satsang fellowship, and remained ardent devotees for life.
