Goto Naviagation&#x1f447; English Muktanand Swami&rsquo;s Kirtan in Raag Bihag and the Historic First Question of Vachanamrut in Gadhada It was the auspicious and historic night of Magshar Sud 6, Samvat 1876 (circa November 1819). Approximately four and a half hours of the night had passed—around 9:30 to 10:00 PM. In Gadhada, outside the sacred Akshar Ordi, Shreeji Maharaj was relaxing upon a cushioned cot, surrounded by eight to ten close parshads (attendants). The devotees were relishing the sublime beauty of His divine countenance while engaged in pleasant spiritual reflections.
Amidst the tranquil quietude of the night, the soul-stirring notes of a devotional hymn sung in the classical Raag Bihag drifted over from the sadhus’ living quarters. Hearing the melodious devotion, Shreeji Maharaj remarked: &ldquo;It seems Sadguru Muktanand Swami is singing a kirtan!&rdquo;
The attendants listened closely and submitted, &ldquo;Maharaj, that does not sound like Muktanand Swami’s voice; it sounds like someone else.&rdquo;
To verify, Shreeji Maharaj dispatched Devji Bhagat from the village of Pachham to investigate. Devji Bhagat walked across to the sadhus&rsquo; residence and asked who was singing. Hearing him, Sadguru Brahmanand Swami (Brahmamuni) stepped out onto the veranda and said, &ldquo;It is we who are singing kirtans.&rdquo;
Devji Bhagat returned to Maharaj and reported: &ldquo;O Maharaj! It is Brahmamuni who is singing the hymns.&rdquo;
However, Shreeji Maharaj—the all-knowing Knower of all hearts—smiled knowingly and said: &ldquo;Devji Bhagat, you did not thoroughly observe from within! Come, let all of us go there together and see for ourselves!&rdquo;
Maharaj rose and, accompanied by His retinue, walked to the sadhus&rsquo; quarters. As they entered the inner room, they beheld a magnificent spiritual sight: Sadguru Muktanand Swami, seated in deep contemplative communion with the Lord&rsquo;s divine form, his eyes half-closed in pure devotional ecstasy, was pouring his heart out in serene verses sung in the nocturnal Raag Bihag!
Seeing Shreeji Maharaj personally arrive in their midst at that hour of the night, all the ascetics rose in joyous reverence. They swiftly spread an exquisite embroidered cloth (chaklo) upon a seat and lovingly requested Maharaj to be seated. Muktanand Swami and the accompanying sadhus prostrated at Maharaj&rsquo;s sacred feet and sat respectfully on the floor surrounding Him.
Shreeji Maharaj was thoroughly pleased by the depth of Muktanand Swami&rsquo;s unbroken remembrance and inner bliss. Taking advantage of the profound spiritual atmosphere, Sadguru Muktanand Swami folded his hands and humbly posed the immortal first question of the Vachanamrut: &ldquo;How should a devotee of God understand spiritual principles so that no worldly disruption can disturb his inner peace&hellip;?&rdquo;
From this divine encounter sprang forth the sacred stream of Shree Vachanamrut (Gadhada First Section, Discourse 1), opening the floodgates of supreme spiritual wisdom that continues to enlighten and liberate seekers across the ages.
