Goto Naviagation&#x1f447; English The Sacred Footprints of Pithvajal Temple and the Resolute Faith of Khoja Brothers Adam and Makan This profound historical episode dates back approximately two hundred and twenty-five years. During that era, Amreli was governed under the Gaekwad princely administration. Two brothers belonging to the Khoja community—named Adam and Makan—were employed in the official government service of collecting agricultural revenues across the rural Kharapat villages. During their travels, they repeatedly heard people speaking of an extraordinary spiritual master named Swaminarayan, whom thousands of devotees revered as the manifest Khuda (Supreme Divinity).
To test the authenticity of these claims with their own eyes, both brothers resolved to mount their fast steeds and ride directly to Gadhpur. As they rode toward Gadhada, they mutually agreed upon an intimate mental test: &ldquo;If Swaminarayan is genuinely the omniscient Khuda, then the moment we reach His assembly, He must call us out by our individual names, honor us with utmost respect, and unprompted, ask us to surrender the rare black-and-red bordered wool blanket (dhabli) that we have kept concealed with us!&rdquo;
Upon arriving at the Darbargadh of Dada Khachar in Gadhpur, they tied their horses near the sacred neem tree in the courtyard. Before they could even announce their arrival, Shreeji Maharaj—presiding over a vast gathering—raised His hand from the dais, waved toward them, and called out aloud: &ldquo;Come forward, Adam! Come forward, Makan! Welcome to our assembly!&rdquo;
Maharaj welcomed both brothers with boundless warmth, seated them with honor in the front row, adorned them with fragrant flower garlands, and arranged rich fodder and grain for their exhausted steeds. Then, with a knowing smile, Maharaj looked straight at them and requested: &ldquo;Pray surrender to Us that special wool blanket with black and red borders that you have concealed and brought for Us!&rdquo;
Beholding every single detail of their private thoughts fulfilled to the letter, both brothers were utterly stunned and overwhelmed with divine awe. Their hearts experienced celestial peace, and their conviction solidified beyond any shadow of doubt that Bhagwan Swaminarayan is indeed the omniscient, manifest Supreme Lord. They resided in Gadhpur for several days, deeply captivated by the immaculate moral conduct, purity, and universal love of the sadhus, and formally embraced initiation as devout Satsangis.
Thereafter, the two brothers took turns visiting Gadhpur regularly. Whenever touring groups of sadhus graced the villages of Nagher and Amreli, Adam and Makan made it their personal mission to attend their spiritual discourses in villages like Amreli, Pithvajal, Gokharvala, and Samadhiyala. Their exemplary devotion is immortalized by Sadguru Nishkulanand Swami in Chapter 114 of the epic scripture Bhakta Chintamani:
&ldquo;Amrelie Punjo Panjaan, Makan Aamad Khoja Sujaan&rdquo;
(In Amreli flourished Punja Panjan, and the wise and enlightened Khoja brothers Makan and Adam&hellip;)
In later years, the brothers settled in the village of Pithvajal. During a severe regional drought, a stalwart devotee named Khimabapa Dobariya invited Sadguru Gunatitanand Swami and Sadguru Krupanand Swami to Pithvajal for a four-month spiritual stay (chaturmas), during which Adam and Makan served the holy assembly with exemplary dedication.
On one occasion, Adam and Makan carried a large basket brimming with fragrant mogra flower garlands from Amreli to Gadhpur as a personal loving offering for Shreeji Maharaj. Pleased beyond measure with their spotless devotion and unshakeable surrender, Shreeji Maharaj blessed them by imprinting His holy Charnarvind (sacred footprints) on a cloth. These venerable relics of divine prasad remain enshrined to this day in the Swaminarayan temple at Pithvajal, continuing to bless pilgrims with their sanctifying presence.
- From reflections on the devotees of Shri Bhaktachintamani&hellip;
