Goto Naviagation&#x1f447; English The Mangrol Samadhi Era: The Brothers Who Plugged Their Ears at the Merchant&rsquo;s Shop and Maharaj&rsquo;s Witty Lesson During the remarkable era of the Samadhi Prakaran in the port town of Mangrol, the spiritual atmosphere was charged with transcendental power. Whenever Shreeji Maharaj&rsquo;s royal procession passed through the marketplace, anyone who caught a glimpse of His divine countenance or even heard the resonant sound of His kettle-drums and the devotees’ chants would instantly fall into a deep spiritual trance (samadhi). Fearful that falling into samadhi would bring business to a standstill and leave their merchandise unguarded, worldly shopkeepers and merchants would hurriedly slam shut their wooden shopfront shutters the moment they heard the procession approaching.
One day, two simple farmer brothers arrived from a neighboring village with bullock carts full of grain to conduct business at a local merchant’s (Vaniya&rsquo;s) shop. Right in the middle of their transaction, a shout echoed down the street: &ldquo;Bhagwan Swaminarayan&rsquo;s procession is arriving!&rdquo;
The terrified shopkeeper began pulling down his shutters and frantically warned the naive farmer brothers: &ldquo;Quick! If you fall into samadhi, your bullocks and merchandise will be lost! Stick your fingers tightly into both ears, tuck your heads deep between your knees, and crouch down on the floor without making a sound so that not even an echo of the chanting penetrates your ears!&rdquo;
The frightened brothers obediently jammed their fingers into their ears, bowed their heads against their knees, and crouched motionless in the corner. Outside, Shreeji Maharaj&rsquo;s majestic procession swept past the street with joyful devotional fervor. Only after the singing faded in the distance did the merchant reopen his shop: &ldquo;Phew! The danger has passed; you may come out now!&rdquo; Finishing their business, the brothers loaded their carts and went home.
Years rolled by. Eventually, through the divine company of pure sadhus, the two brothers were exposed to the glory of Bhagwan Swaminarayan, grasped His supreme identity as the manifest Almighty, and embraced the Satsang fellowship as dedicated devotees.
On one occasion, they traveled with a large pilgrim congregation (sangh) to Gadhada to have darshan of Shreeji Maharaj. Standing before Maharaj with folded hands, they pleaded earnestly: &ldquo;O merciful Maharaj! Please shower Your divine grace upon us and grant us transcendental samadhi!&rdquo;
The omniscient Shreeji Maharaj instantly recognized the two brothers from that afternoon in Mangrol. With a divine twinkle in His eye and a hearty laugh, Maharaj delivered a classic Kathiyawadi proverb filled with humorous spiritual wisdom: &ldquo;Dear brothers! &lsquo;Rai na bhav raate gaya&rsquo;—the price of mustard seed vanished with the night that has passed! That divine opportunity cannot be recalled now. What could have been effortlessly attained at that golden moment (taane) cannot be bought today even with boundless wealth (naane)!&rdquo;
Hearing Maharaj’s witty retort, the entire assembly erupted in laughter, while the two brothers learned an unforgettable lesson regarding the supreme importance of seizing the manifest divine opportunity when God walks upon the earth.
